302:
** ἄν**, [ᾰ],
_Epic dialect_, Lyric poetry, _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}}, _Attic dialect_; also κεν) _Epic dialect_, _Aeolic dialect_, Thess., κᾱ _Doric dialect_, _Boeotian dialect_, El.; the two combined in _Epic dialect_ (below {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 11.2) and 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}}, εἰκ ἄν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).6.2, 15 (4th c.BC)}}:—modal Particle used with Verbs to indicate that the action is limited by circumstances or defined by conditions. In {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} κε is four times as common as ἄν, in Lyric poetry about equally common. No clear distinction can be traced, but κε as an enclitic is somewhat less emphatic; ἄν is preferred by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} in negative clauses, κε (ν) with the relative.
**A** In Simple Sentences, and in the Apodosis of Compound Sentences; here ἄν belongs to the Verb, and denotes that the assertion made by the Verb is dependent on a condition, expressed or implied: thus ἦλθεν **he came,** ἦλθεν ἄν **he would have come** (under conditions, which may or may not be defined), and so **he might have come;** ἔλθοι **may he come,** ἔλθοι ἄν **he would come** (under certain conditions), and so **he might come.**
**A.I** WITH INDICATIVE:
**A.I.1** with historical tenses, generally _imperfect_ and _aorist_, less frequently _pluperfect_, never _perfect_, see below,
**A.I.1.a** most frequently in apodosis of conditional sentences, with protasis implying nonfulfilment of a past or present condition, and _apodosis_ expressing what **would be** or **would have been** the case if the condition **were** or **had been** fulfilled. The _imperfect_ with ἄν refers to continued action, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always in past time, except perhaps καί κε θάμ᾽ ἐνθάδ᾽ ἐόντες ἐμισγόμεθ᾽{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4. 178}}; later also in _present_ time, first in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 905}}; πολὺ ἂν θαυμαστότερον ἦν, εἰ ἐτιμῶντο it **would be** far more strange if they were honoured, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 489a}}; οὐκ ἂν νήσων ἐκράτει, εἰ μή τι καὶ ναυτικὸν εἶχεν he **would** not **have** been master of islands if he had not had also some naval power, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9}}. The _aorist_ strictly refers only to past time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 11.24, etc.}}; εἰ τότε ταύτην ἔσχε τὴν γνώμην, οὐδὲν ἂν ὧν νυνὶ πεποίηκεν ἔπραξεν if he had then come to this opinion, he **would have** accomplished nothing of what he has now done, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.5, +others}}, but is used idiomatically with Verbs of saying, answering, etc., as we say **I should have** said, εἰ μὴ πατὴρ ἦσθ᾽, εἶπον ἄν σ᾽ οὐκ εὖ φρονεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 755, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 199d, “Euthphr.” 12d, etc.: the}}_pluperfect_ refers to completed actions, as ὃ εἰ ἀπεκρίνω, ἱκανῶς ἂν ἤδη παρὰ σοῦ τὴν ὁσιότητα ἐμεμαθήκη I **should have** already learnt..,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 14c; εἰ ὁ ἀνὴρ ἀπέθανεν, δικαίως ἂν ἐτεθνήκει 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.2.3.}}
**A.I.1.b** the protasis is frequently understood: ὑπό κεν ταλασίφρονά περ δέος εἷλεν fear **would have** seized even the stout-hearted (had he heard the sound), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.421}}; τὸ γὰρ ἔρυμα τῷ στρατοπέδῳ οὐκ ἂν ἐτειχίσαντο they **would** not **have** built the wall (if they had not won a battle), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.11}}; πολλοῦ γὰρ ἂν ἦν ἄξια for (if that were so) they **would be** worth much, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 374d}}; οὐ γὰρ ἦν ὅ τι ἂν ἐποιεῖτε for there was nothing which you **could have** done, i. e. **would have** done (if you had tried), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.43.}}
**A.I.1.c** with no definite protasis understood, to express what **would have been likely to happen,** or **might have happened** in past time: ἢ γάρ μιν ζωόν γε κιχήσεαι, ἤ κεν Ὀρέστης κτεῖνεν ὑποφθάμενος for either you will find him alive, or else Orestes **may** already **have** killed him before you, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.546}}; ὃ θεασάμενος πᾶς ἄν τις ἀνὴρ ἠράσθη δάϊος εἶναι every man who saw this (the 'Seven against Thebes') **would have** longed to be a warrior, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1022}}; especially with τάχα, which see, ἀλλ᾽ ἦλθε μὲν δὴ τοῦτο τοὔνειδος τάχ᾽ ἂν ὀργῇ βιασθὲν μᾶλλον ἢ γνώμῃ φρενῶν, i. e. it **might** perhaps **have** come, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 523}}; τάχα ἂν δὲ καὶ ἄλλως πως ἐσπλεύσαντες (i.e. διέβησαν) and they **might** also perhaps **have** crossed by sea (to Sicily) in some other way, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.2, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 265b.}}
**A.I.1.d** ἄν is frequently omitted in apodosi with Verbs expressing obligation, propriety, or possibility, as ἔδει, ἐχρῆν, εἰκὸς ἦν, etc., and sometimes for rhetorical effect, εἰ μὴ.. ᾖσμεν, φόβον παρέσχεν it **had** caused (for it **would have** caused) fear, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1113. This use becomes more common in later Gk.}}
**A.I.2** with _future_ _indicative_:
**A.I.2.a** frequently in _Epic dialect_, usually with κεν, rarely ἄν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.167, 22.66, indicating a limitation or condition, ὁ δέ κεν κεχολώσεται ὅν κεν ἵκωμαι}} and he **will likely be** angry to whom-**soever** I shall come, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.139}}; καί κέ τις ὧδ᾽ ἐρέει and in that case men will say,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.176; ἐγὼ δέ κέ τοι καταλέξω 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.80}}; so in Lyric poetry, μαθὼν δέ τις ἂν ἐρεῖ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.68, compare “I.” 6(5).59.}}
**A.I.2.b** rarely in codices of _Attic dialect_ Prose writers, σαφὲς ἂν καταστήσετε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.140; οὐχ ἥκει, οὐδ᾽ ἂν ἥξει δεῦρο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 615d, compare “Ap.” 29c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.13; uncertain in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.174: in later Prose, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “V A” 2.21, S 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “M.” 9.225: also in Poetry, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 484, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1313; οὐκ ἂν προδώσω 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.36 (correlated -δοίην):— for ἄν with}}_future_ _infinitive_ and _participle_ see below
**A.II** WITH SUBJUNCTIVE, only in _Epic dialect_, the meaning being the same as with the _future_ _indicative_ {{Refs|(1.2a), frequently with _1st pers._, as εἰ δέ κε μὴ δώῃσιν, ἐγὼ δέ κεν αὐτὸς ἕλωμαι in that case}}**I will** take her myself, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.324}}; πείθευ, ἐγὼ δέ κέ τοι εἰδέω χάριν obey and **if so** I will be grateful, {{Refs|14.235 (the _subjunctive_ is always introduced by δέ in this usage)}}; also with other persons, giving emphasis to the future, οὐκ ἄν τοι χραίσμῃ κίθαρις{{Refs|3.54, +others}}
**A.III** WITH OPTATIVE (never _future_, rarely _perfect_ πῶς ἂν λελήθοι [με]; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 3.6):}}
**A.III.a** in apodosis of conditional sentences, after protasis in _optative_ with εἰ or some other conditional or relative word, expressing a _future_ condition: ἀλλ᾽ εἴ μοί τι πίθοιο, τό κεν πολὺ κέρδιον εἴη{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.28; οὐ πολλὴ ἂν ἀλογία εἴη, εἰ φοβοῖτο τὸν θάνατο; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 68b:—in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} _present_ and _aorist_ _optative_ with κε or ἄν are sometimes used like _imperfect_ and _aorist_ _indicative_ with ἄν in Attic, with either regular _indicative_ or another _optative_ in the protasis: καί νύ κεν ἔνθ᾽ ἀπόλοιτο.. εἰ μὴ.. νόησε κτλ., i. e. he **would have** perished, had she not perceived, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.311, compare 5.388, 17.70}}; εἰ νῦν ἐπὶ ἄλλῳ ἀεθλεύοιμεν, ἦ τ᾽ ἂν ἐγὼ.. κλισίηνδε φεροίμην if we were now contending in another's honour, I **should** now carry.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 23.274: so rarely in Trag., οὐδ᾽ ἂν σὺ φαίης, εἴ σε μὴ κνίζοι λέχος (for εἰ μὴ ἔκνιζε) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 568.}}
**A.III.b** with protasis in _present_ or _future_, the _optative_ with ἄν in apodosi takes a simply future sense: φρούριον δ᾽ εἰ ποιήσονται, τῆς μὲν γῆς βλάπτοιεν ἄν τι μέρος they **might perhaps** damage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142, compare 2.60, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 25b, “R.” 333e; ἢν οὖν μάθῃς.. οὐκ ἂν ἀποδοίην 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 116, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.26, +others}}
**A.III.c** with protasis understood: φεύγωμεν· ἔτι γάρ κεν ἀλύξαιμεν κακὸν ἦμαρ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.269}}; οὔτε ἐσθίουσι πλείω ἢ δύνανται φέρειν· διαρραγεῖεν γὰρ ἄν for (if they should do so) they **would** burst, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.21}}; τὸν δ᾽ οὔ κε δύ᾽ ἀνέρε.. ἀπ᾽ οὔδεος ὀχλίσσειαν two men **could** not heave the stone from the ground, i. e. **would** not, if they should try, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.447; οὐδ᾽ ἂν δικαίως ἐς κακὸν πέσοιμί τι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 240, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.8: in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} sometimes with _reference_ to past time, Τυδεΐδην οὐκ ἂν γνοίης ποτέροισι μετείη{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.85.}}
**A.III.d** with no definite protasis implied, in potential sense: ἡδέως δ᾽ ἂν ἐροίμην Λεπτίνην but I **would** gladly ask Leptines, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.129}}; βουλοίμην ἄν I **should** like, Latin _velim_ (but ἐβουλόμην ἄν I **should** wish, if it were of any avail, **vellem**); ποῖ οὖν τραποίμεθ᾽ ἄ; which way then **can** we turn? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 290a}}; οὐκ ἂν μεθείμην τοῦ θρόνου I **will** not give up the throne, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 830}}; idiomatically, referring to the past, αὗται δὲ οὐκ ἂν πολλαὶ εἶεν but these **would** not (on investigation) prove to be many, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9}}; εἴησαν δ᾽ ἂν οὗτοι Κρῆτες these **would** be (i. e. **would have** been) Cretans, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2: used in order to soften assertions by giving them a less positive form, as οὐκ ἂν οὖν πάνυ γέ τι σπουδαῖον εἴη ἡ δικαιοσύνη}} i.e. it **would** not **prove to be,** etc. (for, it **is** not, etc.), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “R.” 333e.}}
**A.III.e** in questions, expressing a wish: τίς ἂν θεῶν.. δοί;{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1100, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1448; πῶς ἂν θάνοιμ; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 389: hence (with no question)}} as a mild command, exhortation, or entreaty, τλαίης κεν Μενελάῳ ἐπιπροέμεν ταχὺν ἰόν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.94}}; σὺ μὲν κομίζοις ἂν σεαυτὸν ᾗ θέλεις you **may** take yourself off (milder than κόμιζε σεαυτόν), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 444}}; χωροῖς ἂν εἴσω you **may** go in, {{Refs|“El.” 1491; κλύοις ἂν ἤδη, Φοῖβε hear me now, Phoebus, [prev. work] 637}}; φράζοις ἄν, λέγοις ἄν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 23c, 48b.}}
**A.III.f** in a protasis which is also an apodosis: εἴπερ ἄλλῳ τῳ ἀνθρώπων πειθοίμην ἄν, καὶ σοὶ πείθομαι if I **would** trust any (other) man (if he gave me his word), I trust you, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prt.” 329b}}; εἰ μὴ ποιήσαιτ᾽ ἂν τοῦτο if you **would** not do this (if you could), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.18, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.5.3, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.4.16.}}
**A.III.g** rarely omitted with _optative_ in apodosis: ῥεῖα θεός γ᾽ ἐθέλων καὶ τηλόθεν ἄνδρα σαώσαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.231, compare 14.123, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.303}}; also in Trag., θᾶσσον ἢ λέγοι τις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1186; τεὰν δύνασιν τίς.. κατάσχο; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 605.}}
**A.III.h** ἄν with _future_ _optative_ is probably always corrupt (compare {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.2b), as τὸν αὐτὸν ἂν ἐπαινέσοι (ἐπαινέσαι Bekk.) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 719e}}; εἰδὼς ὅτι οὐδέν᾽ ἂν καταλήψοιτο (οὐδένα Bekk.) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.22.}}
**A.IV** WITH _infinitive_ and _participle_ (sometimes _adjective_ equivalent to _participle_, τῶν δυνατῶν ἂν κρῖναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 577b) representing _indicative_ or _optative_:}}
**A.IV.1** _present_ _infinitive_ or _participle_:
**A.IV.1.a** representing _imperfect_ _indicative_, οἴεσθε τὸν πατέρα.. οὐκ ἂν φυλάττει; do you think he **would** not **have** kept them safe? (οὐκ ἂν ἐφύλαττεν), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.35}}; ἀδυνάτων ἂν ὄντων [ὑμῶν] ἐπιβοηθεῖν when you **would have** been unable, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.73, compare 4.40.}}
**A.IV.1.b** representing _present_ _optative_, πόλλ᾽ ἂν ἔχων (representing ἔχοιμ᾽ ἄν) ἕτερ᾽ εἰπεῖν παραλείπω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.258, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.18: with _Article_, τὸ ἐθέλειν ἂν ἰέναι ἄκλητος ἐπὶ δεῖπνον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174b.}}
**A.IV.2** _aorist_ _infinitive_ or _participle_:
**A.IV.2.a** representing _aorist_ _indicative_, οὐκ ἂν ἡγεῖσθ᾽ αὐτὸν κἂν ἐπιδραμεῖ; do you not think he **would** even **have** run thither? (καὶ ἐπέδραμεν ἄν), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.56}}; ἴσμεν ὑμᾶς ἀναγκασθέντας ἄν we know you **would have** been compelled, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.76, compare 3.89}}; ῥᾳδίως ἂν ἀφεθείς when he **might** easily **have** been acquitted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.4.}}
**A.IV.2.b** representing _aorist_ _optative_, οὐδ᾽ ἂν κρατῆσαι αὐτοὺς τῆς γῆς ἡγοῦμαι I think they **would** not even be masters of the land (οὐδ᾽ ἂν κρατήσειαν), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.37, compare 2.20}}; ὁρῶν ῥᾳδίως ἂν αὐτὸ ληφθέν (ληφθείη ἄν) {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.42}}; οὔτε ὄντα οὔτε ἂν γενόμενα, i.e. things which are not and never **could** happen (ἃ οὔτε ἂν γένοιτο), {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.38.}}
**A.IV.3** _perfect_ _infinitive_ or _participle_ representing:
**A.IV.3.a** _pluperfect_ _indicative_, πάντα ταῦθ᾽ ὑπὸ τῶν βαρβάρων ἂν ἑαλωκέναι (φήσειεν ἄν) he **would** say that all these **would have** been destroyed by the barbarians (ἑαλώκη ἄν), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.312.}}
**A.IV.3.b** _perfect_ _optative_, οὐκ ἂν ἡγοῦμαι αὐτοὺς δίκην ἀξίαν δεδωκέναι, εἰ.. καταψηφίσαισθε I do not believe they **would** (then) have suffered (δεδωκότες ἂν εἶεν) punishment enough, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 27.9.}}
**A.IV.4** _future_ _infinitive_ or _participle_, never in _Epic dialect_, and probably always corrupt in _Attic dialect_, νομίζων μέγιστον ἂν σφᾶς ὠφελήσειν (to be read -ῆσαι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.82, compare 6.66, 8.25, 71}}; _participle_ is still more exceptional, ὡς ἐμοῦ οὐκ ἂν ποιήσοντος ἄλλα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30c (codices), compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.342 (variant)}}; both are found in later Gk., νομίσαντες ἂν οἰκήσειν οὕτως ἄριστα{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.30.8, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marcellus” 15, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.3}}; with _participle_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 14.1, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 26, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 62.21, uncertain reading in 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 6.6.5.}}
**B** IN DEPENDENT CLAUSE{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**B.I** In the protasis of conditional sentences with εἰ, regularly with the subjunctive. In Attic εἰ ἄν is contracted into ἐάν, ἤν, or ἄν (ᾱ) (which see): {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has generally εἴ κε (or αἴ κε), sometimes ἤν, once εἰ δ᾽ ἄν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.288, twice εἴπερ ἄν 5.224, 232. The protasis expresses either future condition (with apodosis of _future_ time) or general condition (with apodosis of repeated action): εἰ δέ κεν ὣς ἔρξῃς καί τοι πείθωνται Ἀχαιοί, γνώσῃ ἔπειθ᾽ ὅς.. if thus thou shalt do.., [prev. work] 2.364}}; ἢν ἐγγὺς ἔλθῃ θάνατος, οὐδεὶς βούλεται θνῄσκειν if death (ever) come near.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 671.}}
**B.I.2** in relative or temporal clauses with a conditional force; here ἄν coalesces with ὅτε, ὁπότε, ἐπεί, ἐπειδή, compare ὅταν, ὁπόταν, ἐπήν or ἐπάν (_Ionic dialect_ ἐπεάν), ἐπειδάν: {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has ὅτε κε (sometimes ὅτ᾽ ἄν), ὁππότε κε (sometimes ὁπότ᾽ ἄν or ὁππότ᾽ ἄν), ἐπεί κε (ἐπεὶ ἄν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.412)}}, ἐπήν, εὖτ᾽ ἄ; see also εἰσόκε (εἰς ὅ κε):—τάων ἥν κ᾽ ἐθέλωμι φίλην ποιήσομ᾽ ἄκοιτιν whomsoever of these I **may** wish.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.397}}; ὅταν δὴ μὴ σθένω, πεπαύσομαι when I shall have no strength.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 91}}; ἐχθρὸς γάρ μοι κεῖνος.. ὅς χ᾽ ἕτερον μὲν κεύθῃ ἐνὶ φρεσίν, ἄλλο δὲ εἴπῃ who**ever** conceals one thing in his mind and speaks another, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.312, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.6, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.21. — 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses}}_subjunctive_ in both the above constructions {{Refs|(1 and 2)}} without ἄ; also Trag. and Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 496, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 805}}; μέχρι and πρίν occasionally take _subjunctive_ without ἄν in prose, e.g. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137, 4.16}} (μέχρι οὗ), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 62c, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.60.}}
**B.I.3** in final clauses introduced by relative _adverbs_, as ὡς, ὅπως (of Manner), ἵνα (of Place), ὄφρα, ἕως, etc. (of Time), frequently in _Epic dialect_, σαώτερος ὥς κε νέηαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.32; ὄφρα κεν εὕδῃ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.359; ὅπως ἂν εἰδῇ.. φράσω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 824; ὅπως ἂν φαίνηται κάλλιστος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 198e; μηχανητέον ὅπως ἂν διαφύγῃ “Grg.” 481a (where ὅπως with _future_ _indicative_ is the regular _construction_)}}; also after ὡς in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, Trag., 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.16, +others, once in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.91 (but _future_}} _indicative_ is regular in _Attic dialect_); ἵνα final does not take ἄν or κε except ἵνα εἰδότες ἤ κε θάνωμεν ἤ κεν.. φύγοιμεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.156 (ἵνα}} = **where** in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 405). μή}} = **lest,** takes ἄν only with _optative_ in apodosis, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 631, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.93.}}
**B.II** in _Epic dialect_ sometimes with OPTATIVE as with _subjunctive_ (always κε (ν), except εἴ περ ἂν αὐταὶ Μοῦσαι ἀείδοιεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.597), εἴ κεν Ἄρης οἴχοιτο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.353}}; ὥς κε.. δοίη ᾧ κ᾽ ἐθέλοι that he **might** give her to whomsoever he **might** please,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 2.54: so in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus in final clauses, 1.75, 99:—in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.135 ὥς κέν τις φαίη, κέν}} belongs to Verb in _apodosis_, as in ὡς δ᾽ ἂν ἥδιστα ταῦτα φαίνοιτο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.81.}}
**B.II.2** rarely in **oratio obliqua,** where a _relative_ or temporary word retains an ἄν which it would have with _subjunctive_ in direct form, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 687, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.15; ἐπειδὰν δοκιμασθείην 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.6}}:—similarly after a preceding _optative_, οὐκ ἀποκρίναιο ἕως ἂν.. σκέψαιο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 101d.}}
**B.III** rarely with εἰ and INDICATIVE in protasis, only in _Epic dialect_:
**B.III.1** with _future_ _indicative_ as with _subjunctive_: αἴ κεν Ἰλίου πεφιδήσεται{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.213:—so with _relative_, οἵ κέ με τιμήσουσι 1.175.}}
**B.III.2** with εἰ and a past tense of _indicative_, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, εἰ δέ κ᾽ ἔτι προτέρω γένετο δρόμος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.526}}; so Ζεὺς γάρ κ᾽ ἔθηκε νῆσον εἴ κ᾽ ἐβούλετο Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.174, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1099 (codex R), 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.197.}}
**B.IV** in later Greek, ἄν with relative words is used with INDICATIVE in all tenses, as ὅπου ἂν εἰσεπορεύετο{{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Mark.6.56; ὅσ᾽ ἂν πάσχετε “PFay.” 136 (4th c.AD)}}; ἔνθ᾽ ἂν πέφυκεν ἡ ὁλότης εἶναι{{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 436.19}}; compare ἐάν, ὅταν.
**C** with _imperfect_ and more rarely _aorist_ _indicative_ in ITERATIVE construction, to express elliptically **a condilion fulfilled whenever an opportumty offered;** frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (not in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus or 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus), κλαίεσκε ἂν καὶ ὀδυρέσκετο}} she **would** (i. e. **used to**) weep and lament, 3.119; εἶτα πῦρ ἂν οὐ παρῆν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 295}}; εἴ τινες ἴδοιεν.., ἀνεθάρσησαν ἄν whenever they saw it, on each occasion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.71; διηρώτων ἂν αὐτοὺς τί λέγοιεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22b:}}_infinitive_ representing _imperfect_ of this _construction_, ἀκούω Λακεδαιμονίους τότε ἐμβαλόντας ἂν.. ἀναχωρεῖν, i. e. I hear they **used to** retire (ἀνεχώρουν ἄν), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.48.}}
**D** GENERAL REMARKS:
**D.I** POSITION OF ἄν.
**D.I.1** in A, when ἄν does not coalesce with the _relative_ word (as in ἐάν, ὅταν), it follows directly or is separated only by other particles, as μέν, δέ, τε, γάρ, καί, νυ, περ,, etc.; as εἰ μέν κεν.. εἰ δέ κε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.281- 4}}; rarely by τις, as ὅποι τις ἄν, οἶμαι, προσθῇ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.14:—in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}} two such Particles may precede κε, as εἴ περ γάρ κεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.355, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.123}}; εἰ γάρ τίς κε, ὃς μὲν γάρ κε, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 280, 357}}; rarely in Prose, ὅποι μὲν γὰρ ἄν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.45; ὁπότερος οὖν ἄν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1420: also ὁπόσῳ πλέον ἄν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 647e, compare 850a; ὅπου τὸ πάλαι λεγόμενον ἂν γίγνηται 739c.}}
**D.I.2** in apodosis, ἄν may stand either next to its Verb (before or after it), or after some other emphatic word, especially an _interrogative_, a negative (e. g. οὐδ᾽ ἂν εἷς, οὐκ ἂν ἔτι, etc.), or an important Adjective or Adverb; also after a participle which represents the protasis, λέγοντος ἄν τινος πιστεῦσαι οἴεσθ; do you think they **would have** believed it if any one had told them? (εἴ τις ἔλεγεν, ἐπίστευσαν ἄν), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.20.}}
**D.I.3** ἄν is frequently separated from its _infinitive_ by such Verbs as οἴομαι, δοκέω, φημί, οἶδα, etc., οὐκ ἂν οἴει..; frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, “Grg.” 486d, +others}}; καὶ νῦν ἡδέως ἄν μοι δοκῶ κοινωνῆσαι I think that I **should,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.25; οὕτω γὰρ ἄν μοι δοκεῖ ἥ τε πόλις ἄριστα διοικεῖσθαι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.2}}; ἃ μήτε προῄδει μηδεὶς μήτ᾽ ἂν ᾠήθη τήμερον ῥηθῆναι (where ἄν belongs to ῥηθῆναι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.225:—in the phrase οὐκ οἶδ᾽ ἂν εἰ, or οὐκ ἂν οἶδ᾽ εἰ, ἄν belongs not to οἶδα, but to the Verb which follows, οὐκ οἶδ᾽ ἂν εἰ πείσαιμι, for οὐκ οἶδα εἰ πείσαιμι ἄν, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 941, cf. Alcaeus48; οὐκ ἂν οἶδ᾽ εἰ δυναίμην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 26b; οὐκ οἶδ᾽ ἂν εἰ ἐκτησάμην 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.12.}}
**D.I.4** ἄν never begins a sentence, or even a clause after a comma, but may stand first after a parenthetic clause, ἀλλ᾽, ὦ μέλ᾽, ἄν μοι σιτίων διπλῶν ἔδει{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 137.}}
**D.II** REPETITION OF ἄν:—in apodosis ἄν may be used twice or even three times with the same Verb, either to make the condition felt throughout a long sentence, or to emphasize certain words, ὥστ᾽ ἄν, εἰ σθένος λάβοιμι, δηλώσαιμ᾽ ἄν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 333, compare “Ant.” 69, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 340, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.76 (near the end), 2.41, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 31a, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.15; ἀφανεῖς ἂν ὄντες οὐκ ἂν ὑμνήθημεν ἄν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1244, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 739}}; attached to a parenthetical phrase, ἔδρασ᾽ ἄν, εὖ τοῦτ᾽ ἴσθ᾽ ἄν, εἰ.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 1438.}}
**D.II.2** ἄν is coupled with κε (ν) a few times in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, as 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.187, 202, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.361, +others; compare ἤν περ γάρ κ᾽ ἐθέλωσιν variant [prev. work] 18.318.}}
**D.III** ELLIPSIS OF VERB:—sometimes the Verb to which ἄν belongs must be supplied, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only εἰμί, as τάτ᾽ ἔλδεται ὅς κ᾽ ἐπιδευής (i.e. ᾖ) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.481}}; ἀλλ᾽ οὐκ ἂν πρὸ τοῦ (i.e. ἔρρεγκον) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 5}}; τί δ᾽ ἂν δοκεῖ σοι Πρίαμος (i.e. πρᾶξαι), εἰ τάδ᾽ ἤνυσε;{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 935:}} —so in phrases like{πῶς γὰρ ἄν}; and πῶς οὐκ ἄν (i.e. εἴη); also in ὥσπερ ἂν εἰ (or ὡσπερανεί), as φοβούμενος ὥσπερ ἂν εἰ παῖς (i. e. ὥσπερ ἂν ἐφοβήθη εἰ παῖς ἦν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 479a}}; so τοσοῦτον ἐφρόνησαν, ὅσον περ ἂν (i.e. ἐφρόνησαν) εἰ..[{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.48:—so also when κἂν εἰ (={καὶ ἂν εἰ}) has either no Verb in the apodosis or one to which ἄν cannot belong, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 477a, “Men.” 72c}}]; compare κἄν:—so the Verb of a protasis containing ἄν may be understood, ὅποι τις ἂν προσθῇ, κἂν μικρὰν δύναμιν (i. e. καὶ ἐὰν προσθῇ) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.14}}; ὡς ἐμοῦ οὖν ἰόντος ὅπῃ ἂν καὶ ὑμεῖς (i.e. ἴητε) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.6.}}
**D.IV** ELLIPSIS OF ἄν:—when an apodosis consists of several co-ordinate clauses, ἄν is generally used only in the first and understood in the others: πείθοι᾽ ἂν εἰ πείθοι᾽· ἀπειθοίης δ᾽ ἴσως{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1049}}: even when the construction is continued in a new sentence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 352e, compare 439b codices: but ἄν}} is repeated for the sake of clearness or emphasis,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 398a, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.156 (where an _optative_ is implied with the third ὡς): rarely expressed with the second of two co-ordinate Verbs and understood with the first, τοῦτον ἂν.. θαρσοίην ἐγὼ καλῶς μὲν ἄρχειν, εὖ δ᾽ ἂν ἄρχεσθαι θέλειν (i. e. καλῶς μὲν ἂν ἄρχοι, εὖ δ᾽ ἂν θέλοι ἄρχεσθαι) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 669.}}
**B ἄν**, [ᾱ], _Attic dialect_, ={ἐάν},{ἤν}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.46 codices, +others; frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, ἂν σωφρονῇ “Phd.” 61b}}; ἂν θεὸς θέλῃ{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 80d, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.50; ἄν τ᾽.. ἄν τε 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 48.4}}: not common in earlier _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{NT+4th c.BC+|“IG” 1.2a5, 2.179b49, +others: but frequently later, “SIG” 1044.27 (4th-3rd c.BC), “PPetr.” 2p.47 (3rd c.BC), “PPar.” 32.19 (2nd c.BC), “PTeb.” 110.8 (1st c.BC), NT.John.20.23, etc.}}
**C ἄν** or ἀν, Epic form of ἀνά, (which see)
**D ἄν**, shortened from ἄνα, see entry ἀνά G.

303:
** ἀνά** [ᾰνᾰ],
_Aeolic dialect_, Thess., {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, 5th c.AD: Cypria}} ὀν, _preposition_ governing _genitive_, _dative_, and _accusative_ By apocope ἀνά becomes ἄν before dentals, as ἂν τὸν ὀδελό; ἄγ before gutturals, as ἂγ γύαλ; ἄμ before labials, as ἂμ βωμοῖσι, ἂμ πέτραις, etc.; ἀμπεπλεγμένας{{Refs 4th c.AD+|“IG” 5(2).514.10 (4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus).}}
**A** WITH GEN., three times in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”, in phrase ἀνὰ νηὸς βαίνειν}} go **on board** ship, {{Refs|2.416, 9.177, 15.284}}; ἂν τοῦ τοίχου, τᾶς ὁδοῦ, τοῦ ῥοειδίου, {{Refs|“IG” 14.352i40, ii 15,83 (from Halaesa).}}
**B** WITH DAT., **on, upon,** without any notion of motion, _Epic dialect_, Lyric poetry, and Trag. (only Lyric poetry), ἀνὰ σκήπτρῳ **upon** the sceptre, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.15, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.6; ἂμ βωμοῖσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.441; ἀνὰ σκολόπεσσι 18.177; ἀνὰ Γαργάρῳ ἄκρῳ 15.152}}; ἀνὰ ὤμῳ **upon** the shoulder, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.128}}; ἀν ἵπποις, i. e. **in** a chariot, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.41; ἂμ πέτραις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 351 (Lyric poetry); ἀνά τε ναυσὶν καὶ σὺν ὅπλοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 754; ἂγ Κόσσῳ “GDI” 1365 (from Epirus).}}
**C** WITH ACCU{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}, the common usage, implying **motion upwards:**
**C.I** of Place, **up, from bottom to top, up along,** κίον᾽ ἀν᾽ ὑψηλὴν ἐρύσαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.176}}; ἀνὰ μέλαθρον **up to,**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 239; [φλὲψ] ἀνὰ νῶτα θέουσα διαμπερὲς αὐχέν᾽ ἱκάνει 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.547; ἀνὰ τὸν ποταμόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.96}}; ἂν ῥόον **up**-stream, {{Refs|“GDI” 5016.11 (from Gortyn)}}; κρῆς ἂν τὸν ὀδελὸν ἐμπεπαρμένον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 796 (from Megarian)}}; simply, **along,** ἂν τὼς ὄρως{{Refs|“Tab.Heracl.” 2.32.}}
**C.I.2** **up and down, throughout,** ἀνὰ δῶμα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.570}}; ἀνὰ στρατόν, ἄστυ, ὅμιλον,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 384, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.173, etc.; ἂγ γύαλα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 550 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἀνὰ πᾶσαν τὴν Μηδικήν, ἀνὰ τὴν Ἑλλάδα, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96, 2.135, etc.; ὀν τὸ μέσσον 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18.3}}; ἀνὰ τὸ σκοτεινόν **in** the darkness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.22.}}
**C.I.3** _metaphorically_, ἀνὰ θυμὸν φρονέειν, ἀνὰ στόμα ἔχειν, to have **continually in** the mind, **in** the mouth, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.36, 250}}; ἀν᾽ Αἰγυπτίους ἄνδρας **among** them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.286}}; ἀνὰ πρώτους εἶναι to be **among** the first, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.86.}}
**C.II** of Time, **throughout,** ἀνὰ νύκτα **all** night **through,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.80; ἀνὰ τὰς προτέρας ἡμέρας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.223; ἀνὰ τὸν πόλεμον 8.123}}; ἀνὰ χρόνον **in course of** time, {{Refs|1.173, 2.151, 5.27}}; ἀνὰ μέσσαν ἀκτῖνα (i. e. **in** the south) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1247.}}
**C.II.2** **distributively,** ἀνὰ πᾶσαν ἡμέραν day **by** day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37, 130, etc.; ἀνὰ πᾶν ἔτος 1.136, etc.; ἀνὰ πάντα ἔτεα 8.65: also ἀνὰ πρεσβύτᾱτα}} **in order of** age, {{Refs|“Test.Epict.” 4.28.}}
**C.III** distributively with Numerals, κρέα εἴκοσιν ἀν᾽ ἡμιωβολιαῖα 20 pieces of meat **at** half an obol **each,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 554; τῶν ἀν᾽ ὀκτὼ τὠβολοῦ that sell 8}} **for** the obol, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 18}}; ἀνὰ πέντε παρασάγγας τῆς ἡμέρας [they marched] **at the rate of** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5 parasangs a day, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.4}}; ἔστησαν ἀνὰ ἑκατόν μάλιστα ὥσπερ χοροί they stood **in bodies of** about {{Refs|100 men}}**each.** {{Refs|[prev. work] 5.4.12}}; κλισίας ἀνὰ πεντήκοντα companies **at the rate of** {{Refs|50}}**in each,** {{NT|NT.Luke.9.14}}; ἔλαβον ἀνὰ δηνάριον a denarius **apiece, **{{NT|NT.Matt.20.10}}; in doctor's prescriptions, ἀνὰ ὀβολὼ β{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.63, etc.: also ἀνὰ δύο ἥμισυ ζῳδίων}}**amounting to** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2 1/ 2 signs, 4th c.BC: Autolycus Astronomus 1.10}}; **multiplied by,** {{Refs|PPetr. 3p.198.}}
**C.IV** Phrases: ἀνὰ κράτος **up to** the full strength, i. e. **vigorously,** ἀνὰ κράτος φεύγειν, ἀπομάχεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.30, 5.3.12}}; ἀνὰ τὸν αὐτὸν λόγον and ἀνὰ λόγον **proportionately,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 110d; especially in math. sense, [prev. author] “Ti.” 37a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 85a38, etc.}}; ἀνὰ μέσον **in** the midst, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 13, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 531.19; ἀνὰ μέρος}}**by turns,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287n17.}}
**D** WITH NOM. of Numerals, etc., distributively, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Rev.21.21, see reading in 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.11, 12, compare 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “fragment” 50,54.}}
**E** WITHOUT CASE as _adverb_, **thereupon,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} and other Poets:— and with the notion of **spreading all over** a space, **throughout, all over,** μέλανες δ᾽ ἀνὰ βότρυες ἦσαν **all over** there were clusters, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.562, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.343:}} —but ἀνά often looks like an _adverb_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, where really it is only parted from its Verb by tmesis, ἀνὰ δ᾽ ἔσχετ; ἀνὰ δ᾽ ὦρτο (for ἀνῶρτο δέ); ἀνὰ τεύχε᾽ ἀείρας (for τεύχεα ἀναείρας), etc.
**F** IN COMPOSITION (joined with other words),
**F.1** as in C. 1, **up to, upwards, up,** opposed to κατά, as ἀνα-βαίνω, -βλέπω, ἀν-αιρέω, -ίστημι: _poetry_ sometimes doubled, ἀν᾽ ὀρσοθύρην ἀναβαίνειν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.132.}}
**F.2** hence flows the sense of **increase** or **strengthening,** as in ἀνακρίν; though it cannot always be translated, as in Homer's ἀνείρομαι:—in this case opposed to ὑπό.
**F.3** from the notion **throughout** (E), comes that of **repetition** and **improvement,** as in ἀνα-βλαστάνω, -βιόω, -γεννάω.
**F.4** the notion of **back, backwards,** in ἀναχωρέω, ἀνανεύω, etc., seems to come from such phrases as ἀνὰ ῥόον **up,** i. e. **against,** the stream.
**G** ἄνα, written with _anastrophe_ as _adverb_, **up! arise!** ἀλλ᾽ ἄνα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.331, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.13}}:—in this sense the _ultima _ is never elided;{{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare ἀλλ᾽ ἄνα, εἰ μέμονάς γε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.247; ἀλλ᾽ ἄνα ἐξ ἑδράνων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 194.}}
**G.2** _apocopation_ ἄν after ὤρνυτο, ὦρτο, **and up stood.. arose,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.268, 23.837, etc.}}
**G.3** when used as _preposition_ ἀνά never suffers anastrophe.

305:
** ἀναβαίνω**,
_imperfect_ ἀνέβαινον: _future_ -βήσομαι: (for _aorist 1_ see infr. B): _aorist 2_ ἀνέβην, _imperative_ ἀνάβηθι, -βῶ, -βῆναι, -βάς: _perfect_ -βέβηκα:— _middle_, _aorist 1_ -εβησάμην, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers.singular_ -εβήσετο, see infr. B:—_passive_, see below 11.2:— **go up, mount,** with _accusative_ loci, οὐρανόν, ὑπερώϊα ἀ. **go up to** heaven, **to** the upper rooms, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.497, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.302}}; φάτις ἀνθρώπους ἀναβαίνει **goes up among,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 6.29}}; more frequently with _preposition_, ἀ. εἰς ἐλάτην, ἐς δίφρον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.287, 16.657}}; rarely with ἀνά repeated, ἀν᾽ ὀρσοθύρην ἀ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.132; after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, most. frequently with ἐπί, ἀ. ἐπὶ τὰ ὑψηλότατα τῶν ὀρέων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.131: with _dative_, νεκροῖς ἀ.}} **to trample on** the dead, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.493: _metaphorically_, ἐπειδὴ ἐνταῦθα ἀναβεβήκαμεν τοῦ λόγου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 445c.}}
**II** Special usages:
**II.1** **mount** a ship, **go on board,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} mostly _absolutely_; ἐς Τροίην ἀ. **embark** for Troy, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.210; ἀπὸ Κρήτης ἀ. 14.252; ἐπὶ τὰς ναῦς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.44, etc.: _metaphorically_, ἀναβάσομαι στόλον}} I **will mount** a prow, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.62.}}
**II.2** **mount** on horse-back (compare ἀναβάτης), ἀ. ἐφ᾽ ἵππον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.7, compare 7.1.3: _absolutely_, ἀναβεβηκώς}} **mounted;** ἀναβάντες (_absolutely_) ἐφ᾽ ἵππων ἐλάσαι{{Refs|3.3.27}}; ἀ. ἐπὶ τροχόν **mount** on the wheel of torture, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.40.}}
**II.2.b** with _accusative_, ἀ. ἵππον **mount** a horse, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 2:}} —_passive_, [ἵππος] ὁ μήπω ἀναβαινόμενος that **has** not yet **been mounted,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.1}}; ἀναβαθείς **when mounted,**{{Refs|[prev. work] 3.4; ἐν ἵππῳ ἀναβεβαμένῳ [prev. author] “Eq.Mag.” 3.4, compare 1.4.}}
**II.3** of land-journeys, **go up** from the coast into Central Asia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.100, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.2; ἀ. παρὰ βασιλέα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.123b.}}
**II.3.b** **go up** to a temple, {{NT|“PPar.” 47.19, NT.Luke.18.10}}; to a town, {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Matt.20.18, +others, compare “PLond.” 3.1170b. 46 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}; in curses, ἀ. παρὰ Δάματρα πεπρημένος{{Refs|“GDI” 3536.19 (from Cnidus), compare “SIG” 1180.9 ([prev. passage]).}}
**II.3.c** **ascend** to heaven, {{Refs|“Ascens.Is.” 2.16.}}
**II.4** of rivers in flood, **rise,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.13}}; ἀ. ἐς τὰς ἀρούρας **overflow** the fields, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.193.}}
**II.5** of plants, **shoot up,** ἐπὶ δένδρα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.18}}; **climb** on sticks, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.3.2}}; generally, **shoot, spring up,** {{NT|NT.Matt.13.7}}; of hair, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.23.}}
**II.6** in _Attic dialect_, ἀ. ἐπὶ τὸ βῆμα, or ἀ. alone, **mount the tribune, rise to speak,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.66, 21.205, “Prooem.” 56}}; ἀ. εἰς τὸ πλῆθος, εἰς or ἐπὶ τὸ δικαστήριον **come before** the people, **before** the court, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 31c, 40b, “Grg.” 486b}}; ἀ. ἐπὶ τὸν ὀκρίβαντα **mount** the stage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 194b: _absolutely_, ἀνάβαινε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 149; ἀνάβηθι [prev. author] “V.” 963}}; of witnesses in court, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.29.}}
**II.7** of the male, **mount, cover,** ἀ. τὰς θηλέας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.192, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 329; ἀ. ἐπί 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.651, compare 2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus 3}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“Milet.” 3.31 (a).6 (6th c.BC).}}
**II.8** of age, δύο ἀναβεβηκὼς ἔτη τῆς ἡλικίας τῆς ἐμῆς two years **older**.., Ach. Tat.1.7.
**II.9** **ascend to higher knowledge,** ἡ ἀναβεβηκυῖα ἐπιστήμη{{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 15.34, compare 9.30; τὰ ἀναβεβηκότα}}**generalities,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.5.}}
**II.10** with _accusative_, **surpass,** κάλλει τὴν πᾶσαν διακόσμησιν{{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “de Ostentis” 22.}}
**III** of things and events, **come to an end, turn out,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10. ; ἀπό τινος ἀ.}}**result from,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 2.17.}}
**III.b** ἀ. ἐπὶ καρδίαν **enter** into one's heart, of thoughts, {{LXX+NT|LXX.2Ki.12.4, LXX.Jer.3.16, NT.1Cor.2.9, cf. NT.Luke.24.38.}}
**III.2** **come to, pass over to,** ἐς Αεωνίδην ἀνέβαινεν ἡ βασιληΐη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.205, compare 1.109.}}
**IV** **return to the beginning,** of discourse, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 144a}}; **go back,** ἀναβήσεται ἐπὶ τὰς κτίσεις τῶν προγόνων{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ εὑρέσεως” 2.2.}}
**B** _aorist_ ἀνέβησα in causal sense, **make to go up,** especially **put on shipboard,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.143, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.191}}; so in _aorist_ _middle_, νὼ ἀναβησάμενοι **having taken** us **on board** with them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.475: rare in Prose, ἄνδρας ἐπὶ καμήλους ἀνέβησε}} **he mounted** men on camels, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80.}}

306:
** ἀναβάλλω**,
**throw up,** χοῦν ἐξ ὀρύγματος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.90, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.10, “Ostr.” 1399 (1st c.AD)}}; τάφρος ἀναβεβλημένη **foss and dyke,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.5.}}
**2** ἀ. τινὰ ἐπὶ τὸν ἵππον **put on** horseback, **mount** him, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 4.4.4, “Eq.” 6.12}}; of the horse, ἀ. τὸν ἀναβάτην **unseat** his rider, {{Refs|[prev. work] 8.7.}}
**3** ἀ. τὰ ὄμματα **cast up** one's eyes, so as to show the whites, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 876a31; τὰ λευκά 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 222.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Ctesias Historicus “fragment” 20.}}
**4** **cause to spring up,** κρήνην{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.6.2.}}
**5** **lay** bricks, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 2587.59, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 103.}}
**6** **lift, remove** a tumour, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus (?)cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 45.17.6.}}
**7** _passive_, **to be lifted up,** in prayer, εὔχονται σπλάγχνοισι κακῶς ἀναβαλλομένοισι{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Aristeas Epicus 1.}}
**II** **put back, put off,** μηκέτι νῦν ἀνάβαλλε.. ἄεθλον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.584}} (the only place in which {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses the _active_)}}; ἀ. τινά **put off** [with excuses], {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.52; ἀ. τὰ πράγματα 4.14}}; **distract** one's attention, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.24:}} —_passive_, ἀνεβλήθη ἡ ἐκκλησία it **was adjourned,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.45}}; ὥστε.. εἰς τοὺς παῖδας ἀναβληθήσεσθαι τὰς τιμωρίας **will be put off** to the time of the sons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.25; ὑμεναίους οὐκ ἀναβαλλομένους 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.43; compare below 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.}}
**II.2** _perfect_ _participle_ _passive_ ἀναβεβλημένος **slow, measured,** αὔλημα{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 1.1, compare 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.8}}: so in _adverb_ **-μένως** **slowly,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 54.}}
**II.2.b** of style, **diffuse,** τὸ ὕπτιον καὶ ἀ.{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 2.11}}; λέξις ἀ., opposed to συνεστραμμένη, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “τέχναι ῥητορικαί” 2p.540S.}}
**III** like {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 111}}, **put on,** ἀ. τὸ Κρητικόν (a short cloak) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 311 (assuming variant).}}
**IV** **run** a risk (probably _metaphorically_ from dice), ἐγώ σφε θάψω κἀνὰ κίνδυνον βαλῶ{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1033.}}
**B** more frequently in _middle_, **strike up, begin to play** or **sing** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare (ἀναβολή 11), ἀναβάλλετο καλὸν ἀείδειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.155, 8.266, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.20: _absolutely_, ἀναβάλεο 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.77; ἀναβαλοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1269: with _accusative_, εὐχὴν ἀ. τῷ Ἔρωτι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.29.}}
**B.II** **put off, delay** a thing **in which oneself is concerned**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|(see. above 11), μηδ᾽ ἔτι δηρὸν ἀμβαλλώμεθα ἔργον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.436, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 410, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.80, “N.” 9.29, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.85; τὸ μέν τι νυνὶ μὴ λάβῃς, τὸ δ᾽ ἀναβαλοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1139; εἰσαῦθις ἀναβεβλήμεθα “Ec.” 983}}; εἰς τὴν ὑστεραίαν ἀναβαλέσθαι [τὴν δίαιταν] **to adjourn** till the morrow, {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.84, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 234b; ἀ. τινας NT.Act.24.22}}: _absolutely_, **defer payment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.33}}: with _future_ _infinitive_, ἀ. κυρώσειν ἐς τέταρτον μῆνα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.86. ; ἀ. ἐς τρίτην ἡμέρην ἀποκρινέεσθαι 5.49; ἀ. ποιήσειν τὰ δέοντα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.9}}: with _aorist_ _infinitive_, ἀ. ὑποκρίνασθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.8; οὐκέτι ἀνεβάλλοντο μὴ τὸ πᾶν οὐ μηχανήσασθαι 6.88.}}
**B.II.2** **throw off oneself** on another, **refer** a thing to him, τὶ ἐπί τινα{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 15.}}
**B.III** **throw one's cloak up** or **back, throw it over the shoulder,** so as to let it hang in folds, ἀναβάλλεσθαι χλαῖναν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1132: so also ἀναβάλλεσθαι alone, [prev. author] “Ec.” 97; ἀ. ἐπιδέξια 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 175e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1568}}; εἴσω τὴν χεῖρα ἔχοντα ἀναβεβλημένον **with one's cloak thrown up** or **back,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.251; ἀναβεβλ. ἄνω τοῦ γόνατος 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 4.4; compare ἀναβολή 1.2.}}
**B.IV** = above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus IV, ἀναβάλλεσθαι μάχας}} **risk** battles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.49.}}
**B.V** **to be wroth,** {{LXX|LXX.Psa.77(78).21.}}

307:
** ἀναβῐβ-άζω**,
_future_ **-βιβάσω**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 97.43 (assuming variant):}}_aorist_ -εβίβασα:— _middle_, _future_ -βιβάσομαι, _Attic dialect_ **-βιβῶμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Amipsias Comicus 30, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.146:}}_aorist_ -εβιβασάμην: (see. βιβάζω):—causal of ἀναβαίνω, **make to go up, cause to mount,** ἐπὶ τὴν πυρήν, ἐπὶ πύργον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86, 3.75, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.53}}; ἐπὶ τὸν τροχόν, of torture, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.43; κατὰ τὸ ἀκρότατον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.3}}: _metaphorically_, **uphft,** ἐπὶ μετεωροτέραν ἐπίνοιαν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 28.}}
**II** Special usages:
**II.1** ἀ. τινὰ ἐφ᾽ ἵππον **mount** one on horseback, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.63, 4.72, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 6.12; ἐπ᾽ ἅρμα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.180; ἐπὶ τὰ ὀχήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.28.}}
**II.2** ἀ. ναῦν **draw** a ship **up** on land, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 1.1.2.}}
**II.3** _middle_, ἀναβιβάζεσθαίτινας ἐπὶ τὰς ναῦς **have** them **put on board** ship, **embark** for sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.33: _absolutely_, ἀναβιβασάμενοι [prev. work] 35, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.10.}}
**II.4** at Athens, **bring up** to the bar of a court of justice as a witness, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 9.30}}:—so in _middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.24, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18d}}; **bring forward** a fellow-prosecutor, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 13}}; but usually of a culprit, **bring up** his wife and children to raise compassion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.148, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 34c, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.24, 20.34, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 41, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.7, compare 2.146}}: so _active_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Philippum” 9.}}
**II.5** ἀ. ἐπὶ τὴν σκηνήν **bring upon** the stage, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.10.16, 29.19.2.}}
**II.6** ἀ. τὰς τιμάς **raise** the prices, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.10, compare “POxy.” 513.27.}}
**II.7** **promote, advance,** στρατιώτην{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 97.43: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἀ. χώραν}} **advance** a step, {{Refs|94.25}}:—_passive_, ἀναβιβάζεσθαι εἰς τιμήν **ascend** to honour, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 16.}}
**II.8** Grammars, ἀ. τὸν τόνον **throw back** the accent, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 49.15, +others}}; of postpositions, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Synt.” 308.10.}}
**II.9** ἀ. τοὺς φθόγγους **lower, moderate** them, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 2.}}
**II.10** Astron., ὁ ἀναβιβάζων σύνδεσμος **ascending** node, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 4.9, etc.}}; without σύνδεσμος, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 5.105.}}

308:
** ἀναβλέπω**,
_future_ **-βλέψω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.111}} **-βλέψομαι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 563}}: _aorist_ **-έβλεψα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} [same place], etc.:—**look up,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 346; πρὸς τὸ φῶς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 515c; εἰς τὸν οὐρανόν “Ax.” 370b}}: especially as a mark of confidence, ἀ. ὀρθοῖς ὄμμασιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.30}}; ἀ. πρός τινα ἐκ τοῦ ἴσου **look** him **in the face, Cyr.**{{Refs|1.4.12.}}
**2** with _accusative_, **look up at,** φῶς ἀναβλέψεσθε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus [same place]: with _dative_, ἀελίου ἀ. λαμπάσι “Ion” 1467; τοῖς κερτομοῦσι γοργὸν ὣς ἀναβλέπει “Supp.” 322 codices}}
**3** with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἀ. φλόγα **cast up** a glance of fire, {{Refs|Id “Ion” 1263.}}
**II** **recover one's sight,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.111, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 243b, NT.John.9.11; πάλιν ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 95, 117.}}
**II.2** **open one's eyes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 621b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.29.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **revive,** ᾧ δῶμ᾽ ἀνέβλεφ᾽{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1308.}}

309:
** ἀνάβλεψις**, εως, ἡ,
**looking up, seeing,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 247b8.}}
**II** **recovery of sight,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Isa.61.1, cf. NT.Luke.4.18.}}

310:
** ἀναβο-άω**,
_future_ **-ήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 465}}, _Doric dialect_ **-άσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 639}}: _aorist_ ἀνεβόησα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.53}}, _Ionic dialect_ ἀνέβωσα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.10, +others}}, _participle_ ἀμβώσας{{Refs|1.8, 3.38}}:—**cry, shout aloud,** especially in sign of grief or astonishment, ἀμβώσας μέγα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus [same places], compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.69, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1079; οἰκτρὸν ἀνεβόασεν “Hel.” 184}}; of the **war-cry,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.38}}; ἀ. παρεῖναι τοὺς πρώτους” **call out** 'let the front rank pass', {{Refs|“HG” 4.2.22.}}
**2** with _accusative_, τάδ᾽ ἀναβοάσας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 525; ἄχη ἀ.}}**bewail, lament,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 572; Πανὸς ἀναβοᾷ γάμους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 190.}}
**3** with _accusative_ _person_, **call on,** συμμάχους{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1592; Ἀσκληπιόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 639.}}
**4** **cry up, extol,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 98.12.}}

311:
** ἀναβολ-ή**,
_poetry_ ἀμβολή, ἡ: (ἀναβάλλω):
**I** of things:
**I.1** **that which is thrown up, mound of earth, bank,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.5, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.95}}; ἀ. χωμάτων **casting up** of dykes, {{Refs|“Arch.Papyrus” 6.132 (from Denderah)}}; διωρύγων{{Refs|“PAmh.” 2.91.11 (_plural_).}}
**I.2** **that which is thrown back over the shoulder, mantle,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 342c “PPetr.” 3p.48 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Neh.5.13, +others}}; of the **toga,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Nicolaus Damascenus Historicus p.119D.}}: also, **fashion of wearing a cloak,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Somn.” 6.}}
**II** of actions,
**II.1** **striking up, prelude** on the lyre preliminary to singing, ὁπόταν προοιμίων ἀμβολὰς τεύχῃς ἐλελιζομένη, addressed to the lyre, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.4}}; especially of dithyramb, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 5D.}}: hence, **rambling dithyrambic ode,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1385, compare “Pax” 830, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1409b25; compare ἀναβάλλω 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus I.}}
**II.2** **putting off, delaying,** οὐκέτι ἐς ἀναβολὰς ἐποιεῦντο τὴν ἀποχώρησιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.21; ὅ τι μέλλετε.. μὴ ἐς ἀ. πράσσετε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.15}}; οὐκ ἐς ἀμβολάς without **delay,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 270; ἐς μηδεμίαν ἀ. “PAmh.” 2.34i. 5}}; ἐν ταῖς ἀ. τῶν κακῶν ἔνεστ᾽ ἄκη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 93}}; ἐπὶ ἀναβολῇ πρᾶσιν, ὠνὴν ποιεῖσθαι sell, buy on **credit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 915e; ἀναβολήν τινος ποιεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.42; ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 201d; εἰς τὸ γῆρας ἀναβολὰς ποιεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 235.8; δακρύοις.. ἐμποιεῖν ἀ. τῷ πάθει [prev. author] 599}}; ἀναβολὰν λαβόντες ἔτη τρία{{Refs|“IG” 9(2).205.22 (Thess.).}}
**II.2.b** **deferred payment,** εὐχρηστήσας σῖτον ἐπ᾽ ἀναβολῇ Ἀρχ.Ἐφ. 1912.60{{Refs|(from Gonni).}}
**II.3** ἀ. δίκης ἐπὶ τὸν βασιλέα **reference, appeal,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.1.55.}}
**II.4** **lifting,** hence, **removal,** of tumours, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 45.2.6.}}
**III** _intransitive_, **going up, ascent, way up,** ἀ. τῶν Ἄλπεων{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.39.9, etc.; τὴν ἀ. ποιεῖσθαι 50.3.}}
**III.2** **bubbling up,** πομφολύγων{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 936b1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Igne” 16}}; of the Nile, **sources,** ἀμβολαί{{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4924 (from Philae).}}

312:
** ἀναγγέλλω**,
(see. ἀγγέλλω) **carry back tidings of, report,** τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 661; πάντ᾽ ἀναγγεῖλαι φίλοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 761; τῷ Βρασίδᾳ τὴν ξυνθήκην 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.122, etc.; τι τῷ δήμῳ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1113a9}}; ἐν ἁλία, of valuers, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Tab.Heracl.” 1.118; τι πρός τινα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.67.11}}: with _participle_, **tell of** person doing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 5.6}}:—_passive_, ὡς ἀνηγγέλθη τεθνεώς{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 18.}}
**II** **proclaim,** τοὺς στεφάνους{{Refs|“OGI” 6 (from Scepsis), “SIG” 412.13 (Delph.)}}:—_passive_, of rewards, ἀνηγγέλθαι αὐτῷ ἀργύριον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 10.15.}}

313:
** ἀναγενν-άω**,
**beget anew, regenerate,** {{NT|NT.1Pet.1.3, compare 23}}:—_passive_, probably in {{Refs|“Corpus Hermeticum” 13.1.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **arouse afresh,** ἀ. πάλιν κακά{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.18W.}}

314:
Included with: ** ἀναγιγνώσκω**,
later ἀναγῑνώσκω:
**I** _Epic dialect_ only in _aorist 2_ ἀνέγνων,
**I.1** **know well, know certainly,** οὐ γάρ πώ τις ἑὸν γόνον αὐτὸς ἀνέγνω{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.216, compare 21.205, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.734.}}
**I.1.b** **perceive,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.23.}}
**I.2** **know again, recognize,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.250; once in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}}, **acknowledge, own,** ἀναγνῶναι τοὺς συγγενέας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.91, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.23}}: _aorist_ _passive_ once in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus, εἰ μὲν γὰρ ἔζη πόσις, ἀνεγνώσθημεν ἄν “Hel.” 290.}}
**II** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, _future_ ἀναγνώσομαι uncertain in {{Refs|“GDI” 5075}}: _aorist 2_ ἀνέγνων, Cretan dialect _3rd.pers.plural_ _subjunctive_ **-γνῶντι**{{Refs|“GDI” 5040.43:}}_perfect_ ἀνέγνωκα:—_passive_, _future_ **-γνωσθήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 17.9}}: _aorist_ ἀνεγνώσθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 127d}}: _perfect_ ἀνέγνωσμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.67, etc.}}:—of written characters, **know** them **again,** and so, **read,** first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).1, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 118, 1065, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.49, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.47, etc. (never in Trag.)}}; ἀναγνώσεται (i.e. ὁ γραμματεύς) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.27, etc.; λαβὼν ἀνάγνωθι τὸ ψήφισμα [prev. author] 18.118; λέγε.. καὶ ἀνάγνωθι [prev. author] 19.70; τὴν διαμαρτυρίαν ἀναγνώτω [prev. author] 44.45; ἀ. πρὸς ἐμαυτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 52}}: _absolutely_, οἱ ἀναγιγνώσκοντες **students,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 1, 5th-6th c.AD: Marinus Biographus “Procl.” 15}} ἀ. παρά τινι Ἀριστοτέλους τὰ περὶ ψυχῆς **attend lectures on** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, [prev. work]12:}} —_passive_, τὰ βιβλία τὰ ἀνεγνωσμένα books **read aloud,** hence, **published,** opposed to τὰ ἀνέκδοτα, Lycon cited in {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 5.73.}}
**III** _Ionic dialect_ usage, causal, mostly in _aorist_ ἀνέγνωσα, **induce** one to do a thing, τούτους.. ἀναγνώσας ἕπεσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.106, compare 1.87, 4.158, 6.83, +others}}; _infinitive_ is omitted, ὡς ἀνέγνωσε when **he had persuaded** him, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.68}}: once in _present_, ἀναγιγνώσκεις στρατεύεσθαι βασιλέα{{Refs|[prev. author] 7.10}}; **persuade, convince,** ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 1:}} —_aorist_ _passive_, ἀνεγνώσθην **to be persuaded** to do a thing, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.7 and 236}}: without _infinitive_, ὑπὸ τῆς γυναικὸς ἀναγνωσθείς{{Refs|4.154; χρήμασι ἀ. 6.50}}: _pluperfect_ _passive_, ὡς οὗτοι.. οἱ ἀνεγνωσμένοι ἦσαν{{Refs|8.110}}: rare in _Attic dialect_, ὑπὸ τῶν κυρίων ἀναγιγνωσκόμενον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.7.}}

315:
** ἀναγκ-άζω**,
_future_ **-άσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 337, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.35}} (later _2nd pers.plural_ -ᾶτε{{Refs|“Arch.Papyrus” 6.286)}}: _perfect_ ἠνάγκακα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hipparchus” 232b}}: _pluperfect_ **-ειν**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.28}}: (ἀνάγκη):—**force, compel,** mostly with _accusative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, ἀ. τινὰ κτείνειν, πόλισμα, συνθήκας ποιεῖσθαι, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.11, 98, 6.42}}; δρᾶν, λέγειν, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 256, “OC” 979, etc.}}: so in _passive_, ἠναγκάζοντο ἀμύνεσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.101: without}}_infinitive_, κἄμ᾽ ἀναγκάζεις τάδε (i.e. δρᾶν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1368, compare “OT” 280}}; ἀναγκάζεσθαί τι **to be forced** [to do] a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 242a, 254b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.5.4; ἀ. τινὰ ἐς τὸ πολεμεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23; ἐς τὸ ἔργον [prev. author] 2.75.}}
**2** with _accusative_ _person_ only, **constrain** a person, τὸ συνδρῶν σ᾽ ἀναγκάσει χρέος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 337}}; especially by argument, opposed to ῥητορικῶς ἐλέγχειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 472b}}; δεινοῖς ἠναγκάσθην **I was constrained, tortured,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 221, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 9.2}}; ἠναγκασμένος, ἀναγκασθείς **under compulsion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.22, 8.99; ὑπὸ δεσμῶν ἀναγκασθείς 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.2; φανεροὶ ἦσαν ἀναγκασθησόμενοι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.19.}}
**3** with _accusative_ _of things_ only, **carry through by force,** πόλις ἀναγκάζει τάδε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 595, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.5.5, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1392a27}}; ἠναγκασμένα λάχανα **forced** vegetables, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.21.}}
**4** with _accusative_ _of things_ et _infinitive_, **contend that** a thing **is necessarily** so and so, μὴ ἀνάγκαζε ὃ μὴ καλόν ἐστιν αἰσχρὸν εἶναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 202b, compare “Cra.” 432c, “Tht.” 196b: followed by _conjunction_, οἱ λόγοι ἀναγκάσειαν ἂν ὅτι ἀθάνατον ψυχή “R.” 611b.}}
**5** _absolutely_, **apply compulsion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1304b9 (ἀναγκάζω is a gloss in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 153c.).}}
**6** in surgery, **use force** to reduce dislocations, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 3, 5, +others}}

316:
** ἀναγκ-αῖος**, α, ον,
in _Attic dialect_ also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 554a, etc.}}: **of, with,** or **by force:**
**I** _active_, **constraining, applying force,** μῦθος ἀ. a word **of force,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.399}}; χρειὼ ἀ. **urgent** necessity, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.57}}; ἦμαρ ἀ. day **of constraint,** i.e. life **of slavery,** {{Refs|16.836}}; ἀ. τύχη a doom **imposed by fate,** or **fateful** chance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 485, compare 803}} (but, **fatal** chance, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 48); πᾶν γὰρ ἀ. χρῆμ᾽ ἀνιηρὸν ἔφυ 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 472, compare 297, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 230}}; τῆς ἀρχῆς τῷ ἀ. παροξυνομένους by the **compulsory nature** of our rule, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.99; δεσμὸς ἀ. 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.33}}; ἐξ ἀναγκαίου under **stress of circumstances,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.60.}}
**I.2** **forcible, cogent,** πειθώ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 265d; ἀποδείξεις “Ti.” 40e; διαλλακτὰς πολὺ τῶν ἐμῶν λόγων ἀναγκαιοτέρους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.60}}; τὰ-ότερα τῶν ἀντιγράφων the **more authoritative** copies, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 390.}}
**II** _passive_, **constrained, forced,** twice in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”, πολεμισταὶ ἀ.}} soldiers **perforce,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.499}}; so δμῶες ἀ.{{Refs|[prev. work] 210 (where however 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis explanation it χρειώδεις}} **trusty, serviceable,** see below {{Refs|6).}}
**II.2** **necessary** (physically or morally), οὐκ ἀ. **unnecessary** (on its different senses in philosophy see {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1015a20ff.), ἀ. [ἐστί]}} **it is necessary to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1317, etc.}}; γίνεταί μοι ἀναγκαιότατον, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.65}}; ἀ. κακόν **a necessary** evil, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 651, cf. Hybreasap. 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.2.24}}: also with _infinitive_, ἔνιαι τῶν ἀποκρίσεων ἀναγκαῖαι διὰ μακρῶν τοὺς λόγους ποιεῖσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 449b; ὁδὸν ἀναγκαιοτάτην εἶναι τρέπεσθαι “Sph.” 242b}}; [μαθήματα] ἀναγκαῖα προμεμαθηκέναι **necessary** for us to have learnt them before, {{Refs|“Lg.” 643c.}}
**II.3** τὰ ἀ. **necessaries of life,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 848a; τὰ ἀ. τοῦ βίου 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.40; ἀ. τροφή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.2.}}
**II.3.b** τὰ ἀ. **things necessary to be done,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.6}}; τὰ ἐκ θεοῦ ἀ. the **appointed order of things, HG**[{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.7.33; θεῶν ἀναγκαῖον τόδε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 584 codices: τὸ ἀ., ={ἀνάγκη}, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 200a31.}}]
**II.4** **indispensable,** i. e. **a bare minimum,** frequently in _superlative_, τὸ ἀναγκαιότατον ὕψος **the least** height **that was absolutely necessary,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90}}; ἡ ἀναγκαιοτάτη πόλις **the least that could be called** a city, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 369d; ἐκ τεττάρων ἀναγκαιοτάτων συγκεῖσθαι πόλιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1291a12}}; αὐτὰ τἀναγκαιότατ᾽ εἰπεῖν give **a bare outline** of the facts, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.126 compare 168}}; ἡ ἀ. συγγένεια **the most distant degree** of kinship **recognized by law,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|44.26: less frequently in Posit., οὐδὲ τἀναγκαῖα ἐξικέσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70}}: hence, **scanty, makeshift,** παρασκευή{{Refs|6.37.}}
**II.5** of persons, **connected by necessary** or **natural ties,** i. e. **related by blood,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 574b; ἀ. δόμοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 533; οἱ ἀ.}}**kinsfolk,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.1; ἀ. φίλοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 671; συγγενεῖς καὶ ἀ. ἄνθρωποι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.290; τοὺς συγγενεῖς αὐτοῦ καὶ ἀ. φίλους NT.Act.10.24, compare “PFlor.” 2.142.2 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.6** Astrology texts, **efficacious,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 63.1 (_comparative_): ἀ. γραμμή}} line **of fate,** {{Refs|Cat.Codex Astr. 7.238.}}
**II.7** **costly,** ὄξος{{Refs 5th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1870 (5th c.AD)}}; ἐσθής[{{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see at {βεστιάριον}.}}]
**III** _adverb_ -ως **of necessity, perforce,** ἀ. ἔχει it **must** be so, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.89, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 239, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 723, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 91e, etc.; ἀ. ἔχει μοι ποιέειν ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.140.ά +others}}; ἀ. φέρειν, opposed to ἀνδρείως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64}}; **as best might be,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 69d.}}
**III.2** γελοίως καὶ ἀ. λέγειν **in a narrow sense** (compare {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|11.4, but probably with play on 111.1), [prev. author] “R.” 527a; πτωχῶς μέν, ἀλλ᾽ ἀ. 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 55.2}}:—_superlative_ ἀναγκαιότατα, λέγεις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 40c.}}
**III.3** **strictly,** κελεύειν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 669.41 (1st c.AD).}}
**IV** οἱ ἀ. τόποι **privy** parts, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 113.9.}}
**V** see {ἀναγκαῖον}, τό

318:
** ἀνάγκη**,
_Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ἀναγκαίη, ἡ, **force, constraint, necessity,** κρατερὴ δ᾽ ἐπικείσετ᾽ ἀ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.458}}; ἀναγκαίη γὰρ ἐπείγει{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 85; ἀναγκαίῃ πολεμίζειν 4.300; τίς τοι ἀνάγκη πτώσσει; 5.633; οἷσιν ἀ. (i.e. φυλάσσειν) 10.418, +others: but in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus usually in _dative_ as}}_adverb_, ἀνάγκῃ **perforce, of necessity,** ἀείδειν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.154; φεύγειν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.150}}: in _active_ sense, **forcibly, by force,** ἴσχειν, ἄγειν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.557, 22.353; μνήσασθαι 7.217: strengthened by καί, 10.434; ὑπ᾽ ἀνάγκης 19.156}}; opposed to ἑκόντες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 231a; ὑπ᾽ ἀναγκαίης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.172, +others; ἐξ ἀνάγκης 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 73, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40, etc.; δι᾽ ἀνάγκης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 47e; σὺν ἀνάγκᾳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.51; πρὸς ἀνάγκαν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 569 codices (Lyric poetry), compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 29.2; κατ᾽ ἀνάγκην 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.7: ἀνάγκη ἐστί}} with _infinitive_, **it must be that.., is necessary that**..,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” above quoted; πᾶσα ἀ. ἐστὶ ὗσαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.22; τρέφειν τοὺς τοκέας τοῖσι μὲν παισὶν οὐδεμία ἀ., τῇσι δὲ θυγατράσι πᾶσα ἀ. [prev. work] 35}}: with _dative_ _person_, ἀ. μοι σχεθεῖν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 16, compare “Pers.” 293:}} —in Trag. frequently in answers and arguments, πολλή γ᾽ ἀνάγκη, πολλή᾽ στ᾽ ἀνάγκη, or πολλή μ᾽ ἀνάγκη, with which an _infinitive_ may always be supplied, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1013, “Hec.” 396, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 295; so πᾶσ᾽ ἀνάγκη “El.” 1497, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 441d}}; ἀνάγκη μεγάλη [ἐστί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|] [prev. work] 485e, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.6, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.9; ἐν ἀνάγκῃ ἐστί 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.8: later ἀνάγκην ἔχω}} with _infinitive_, {{NT|NT.Luke.14.18.}}
**2** **necessity** in the philosophical sense, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 94b37, “Metaphorical” 1026b28, “Ph.” 199b34}}; **logical necessity, Metaphorical**{{Refs|1064b33: in _plural_}}**laws of nature,** τίσιν ἀνάγκαις ἕκαστα γίγνεται τῶν οὐρανίων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.11, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 21.}}
**2.b** **natural need,** γαστρὸς ἀνάγκαις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 726, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1075, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 7.1; ὑπ᾽ ἀ. τῆς ἐμφύτου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 458d; ἐρωτικαῖς ἀ. [prev. work], etc.}}
**2.c** ἡ ἀ. τοῦ τόπου the **lie** of the ground as a **necessary condition,** {{Refs|PLille 4.14.}}
**2.d** ἀνάγκη δαιμόνων, αἱ ἐκ θεῶν ἀνάγκαι, **fate, destiny,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1000, 1763}}: frequently personified in Poets, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.30, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 116, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 105, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 256; Ἀνάγκᾳ δ᾽ οὐδὲ θεοὶ μάχονται 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 5.21.}}
**3** **compulsion** exerted by a superior, ἀ. προστιθέναι, ἐπιτιθέναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 9.4, “Lac.” 10.7.}}
**3.b** **violence, punishment,** especially of torture, mostly _plural_, ἐς ἀνάγκας ἄγεσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.25, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.5; προσάγειν τινὶ τὰς ἀνάγκας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99}}; τὰ πρὸς ἀνάγκας ὄργανα instruments of **torture,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.28.2}}: later in _singular_, ἡ ἀ. τῶν βασάνων{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.305e; πρὸς ἀνάγκην}}**under torture,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Publ.” 17: _metaphorically_, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τέχνης” 13}}; δολοποιὸς ἀ., i. e. the stratagem of Nessus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 832; βρόχων πλεκταῖς ἀνάγκαις 4th c.BC: Xenarchus Comicus 1.9.}}
**3.c** **duress, 'force majeure',** ὅρκους οὓς ποιέονται ἐν ἀνάγκῃ ὄντες{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 239}}; **stress** of circumstances, ἀκούσιοι ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82.}}
**3.d** **treatment by mechanical force,** τῶν ἀναγκῶν τινὰ προσφέρειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 15, compare “Art.” 73.}}
**4** **bodily pain, anguish,** κατ᾽ ἀνάγκην ἕρπειν **painfully,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 206 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὑπ᾽ ἀνάγκης βοᾶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 215; ὠδίνων ἀνάγκαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 89 (Lyric poetry)}}: generally, **distress,** ἐν ἀνάγκαις γλυκὺ γίνεται καὶ τὸ σκληρόν{{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 226; frequently in LXX, LXX.Job.15.24, +others; ἡ ἐνεστῶσα ἀ. NT.1Cor.7.26: especially in _plural_, “IG” 12 (7).386.23 (Amorgos, 3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.43, NT.2Cor.6.4, etc.}}
**II** **tie of blood, kindred,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.5.}}
**III** ={ἡ δικαστικὴ κλεψύδρα}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

319:
** ἀναγνωρ-ίζω**,
**recognize,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 258a, “Prm.” 127a, +others}}:—_middle_,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 3.5.5}}: _passive_, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.45.1.}}
**2** in a tragedy, **recognize** or **come to the knowledge** of a person or thing, so as to produce a **dénouement,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1452a36, +others}}
**2.b** **reveal oneself, make oneself known,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 1452b5, +others}}
**2.c** causal, **cause to recognize, reveal oneself to,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.59.}}
**3** **recognize** a rule in a new instance, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 67a24.}}

320:
** ἀνάγν-ωσις**, εως, ἡ,
**recognition,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116.}}
**2** **reading,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 279e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1462a17 (probable reading), “Rh.” 1414a18, etc.: _plural_, Aristeas 283.}}
**2.b** **reading aloud,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.61, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.49, NT.Act.13.15, “SIG” 959.8 (from Chios), 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 642.11}}: in _plural_, **public readings,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 81ce; -ώσεις τῷ θεῷ ποιούμενος “BCH” 31.351 (from Delos).}}
**II** Grammars, in textual criticism, **reading,** ἡ Ἀριστάρχειος ἀ.{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 164.2.}}
**III** ={πραγματεία}, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 3.34.}}
**IV** **persuasion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus (misunderstanding 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116).}}

321:
** ἀνάγω**,
_future_ ἀνάξω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10.θ, etc.:}}_aorist 2_ ἀνήγαγον, etc.: (see. ἄγω):—opposed to κατάγω,
**I** **lead up** from a lower place to a higher, ἐς Ολυμπον{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1347, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 289; πρὸς τὸ ὄρος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.28}}; ἱερὸν ἀ. ξόανον, of the Trojan horse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 525}}; ὁ πέπλος ἀνάγεται εἰς τὴν ἀκρόπολιν {{Refs|Pl “Euthphr.” 6c.}}
**I.2** **lead up to the high sea, carry by sea,** λαὸν ἀνήγαγεν ἐνθάδ᾽ ἀείρας{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.338; γυναῖκ᾽ εὐειδέ᾽ ἀνῆγες ἐξ Ἀπίης γαίης 3.48, compare 6.292; στρατὸν ἐπὶ τὴν Ἑλλάδα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10.θ: but frequently = simple ἄγω}} **conduct, carry to** a place, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.203, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.272}}; ἀ. ναῦν **put** a ship **to sea,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.12, 7.100, etc.}}; ἀνάγειν _absolutely_ in the same sense, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.41, 8.76, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.169:}} —but this is more common in _middle_, see infr. B.I.
**I.3** **take up** from the coast into the interior, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.272}}; especially from Asia Minor into Central Asia, ἀ. παρὰ or ὡς βασιλέα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.119, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.4.6, “An.” 2.6.1, etc.; from Piraeus to Athens, [prev. author] “HG” 2.4.8.}}
**I.4** **bring up,** especially from the dead, ἀ. εἰς φάος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 626; εἰς φῶς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 521c, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 557 (_passive_)}}; τῶν φθιμένων ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1023, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 985}}; κλίνει κἀνάγει πάλιν lays low and **brings up** again, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 131; ἐκ λεχέων ἀ. φάμαν παλαιάν}}**waken up, revive, renew,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).22.}}
**I.5** ἀ. χορόν **conduct** the choir, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 280, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 326, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.104}}; ἀ. θυσίαν, ὁρτήν **celebrate**.., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.48, 60, +others, cf. NT.Act.7.41}}; **sacrifice,** ταύρους{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 764.47 (2nd c.BC).}}
**I.6** **lift up, raise,** κάρα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 866; τὸ ὄμμα ἀ. ἄνω 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 533d}}; ἀ. τὰς ὀφρῦς, ={ἀνασπᾶν}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.975c; ἂν πυκτεύοντες ἀνάγωσιν ἑαυτούς [prev. author] 2.541b.}}
**I.7** ἀ. παιᾶνα **lift up** a paean, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 210}}; ἄναγε πολύδακρυν ἁδονάν, of a song of lamentation, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 126; κωκυτόν “Ph.” 1350.}}
**I.8** ἀ. εἰς τιμήν **raise** to honour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 16; τίμιον ἀ. τινά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1333}}; **elevate,** οἱ εἰς φιλοσοφίαν ἀνάγοντες [ἀστρονομίαν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 529a.}}
**I.9** in various senses, φάρμακα ἀνάγοντα **expectorants,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 3.15}}; ἀ. ὀδόντας **cut** teeth, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aph.” 3.25}}; ἀ. πλῆθος αἵματος **bring up** blood, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cleomenes” 30}}; ἀ. μηρυκισμόν **chew** the cud, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.11.3, +others}}; τὸν Νεῖλον ἀναγέτω **bring** the Nile **up** [over its banks], {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 3; ἀ. φάλαγγα}}**deploy,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Crassus” 23}}: Geometry texts, **draw** a line, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1051a25}}; ἀ. τεταγμένως **erect** as an ordinate, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 2.49}}; in building, **carry** a line of works to a point, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 18: ἀ. ὕδωρ}}**distil,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Synesius Alchemista p.66B.}}
**I.10** μύρια τάλαντ᾽ εἰς τὴν ἀκρόπολιν ἀνήγαγον, i.e. **paid** them **into** the treasury there, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.24.}}
**I.11** **bring up** a prisoner for examination, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.3.11, “OGI” 483.185 (Pergam.), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 40.4.2, NT.Act.12.4.}}
**I.12** **train, rear,** θετὸν υἱόν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.254 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus)}}:—_passive_, εἰς μέτρα ἥβης ἀνηγόμην{{Refs|“IG” 12(7).449 (from Amorgos)}}; of plants, ἀ. ἀμπελῶνας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus (?) “fragment” 1010.}}
**II** **bring back,** ἀνήγαγον αὖθις Ἄργος ἐς ἱππόβοτον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.29, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.401, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.3, etc.}}
**II.2** τὸν λόγον ἐπ᾽ ἀρχὴν ἀ. **carry back, refer** to its principles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 626d; εἰς ἄλλας ἀρχάς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1113b20; εἰς αὑτὸν τὴν ἀρχήν 1113a6, compare “GA” 778b1, +others; εἰς γνωριμώτερον “Metaphorical” 1040b20}}; generally, **refer,** πάντα τοῖς λογισμοῖς εἰς ἀσφάλειαν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 12; εἰς κοινὸν ὄνομα 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 266.13}}; frequently in _passive_, ἀνάγομαι εἴς τι{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 21; ὑπό τι 6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 326.33; ἀπό, ἔκ τινος}}**to be derived from,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 121.25, “Synt.” 23.26}}; ἀ. ἀπό, ἐξ.. **derive one's subsistence from**.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 10.15, 73.11.}}
**II.3** ἀ. τι εἰς τὸν δῆμον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1292a25}}; of persons, ἀ. τινὰ ἐπὶ τὴν συγγραφήν **refer** him to the contract, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.31.}}
**II.4** **reduce** syllogism to another figure, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 29b1}}; **reduce** an argument to syllogism, {{Refs|[prev. work] 46b40, +others}}
**II.5** in Law, **return** a slave sold with an undisclosed defect, εἰς πρατῆρα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 915c, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 15.}}
**II.6** **refer a claimant,** πράτορι ἢ εἰς πόλιν ἔνδικον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Milet.” 3 No.140.42: _absolutely_, ὁ ἔχων ἀναγέτω “Foed.Delph.Pell.” 2A15; ἀ. ὅθεν εἴληφας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.81.}}
**II.7** **rebuild,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 15, “Cam.” 32.}}
**II.8** **restore to its original shape,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus}}**Ep. Dedic.;** τάφρον{{Refs|“PHal.” 1.5.}}
**II.9** **reckon, calculate,** ἀ. τὰς ἡμέρας πρὸς τὸ μαντεῖον{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cimon” 18; χρόνον ἐκ τῶν Ὀλυμπιονικῶν “Num.” 1.}}
**II.10** _intransitive_ (i.e. ἑαυτόν), **withdraw,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.45, etc.}}; ἐπὶ πόδα ἀ. **retreat** facing enemy,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|3.3.69; ἀ. ἐπὶ σκέλος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 383: _metaphorically_, ἄναγε εἰς τοὐπίσω}} perhaps nautical, **put back again,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 528a.}}
**B** _middle_ and _passive_, **put out to sea, set sail** (see.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|above 1.2), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.478, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.137, etc.}}: _future_ ἀνάξεσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.30, etc.; ἀναχθέντες 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.138, 4.152, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 626.}}
**B.2** _metaphorically_, **put to sea,** i. e. **make ready, prepare oneself,** ὡς ἐρωτήσων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 155d, compare “Erx.” 392d.}}
**B.3** in thought, **ascend** to higher unity, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 117.}}

322:
** ἀνα-δείκνῡμι**,
also **ἀναδεικνύω** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.417e}}: Ionic _aorist_ **-έδεξα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (see. below)}}: _perfect_ **-δέδειχα**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.21.3: (see. δείκνυμι)}}:—**lift up and show, exhibit, display,** πύλας ἀναδεικνύναι **display by opening** gates, i.e. **throw wide** the gates, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1458; μυστοδόκος δόμος ἀναδείκνυται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 304}}; ἀναδέξαι ἀσπίδα **hold** up shield **as signal,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.115, 121f}}; ἀνέδεξε σημήιον τοῖς ἄλλοις ἀνάγεσθαι **made** signal for them to put to sea, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.128}}; [Μίλητος] Θαλῆν ἀ., on a statue, epigram cited in {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.34.}}
**II** **notify,** especially **proclaim** any one as elected to office, αὑτὸν ἀναδεδειχὼς βασιλέα{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.48.3}}; ἀ. τινὰ μέγιστον **make** him the greatest man,{{NT|22.4.3; ἀνέδειξεν ἑτέρους ἑβδομήκοντα NT.Luke.10.1}}:—_passive_, ἀναδεδεῖχθαι τὸ ἱερὸν ἄσυλον{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 630.23 (Delph., 2nd c.BC).}}
**II.2** **dedicate,** τῷ Διὶ ταῦρον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 589.6 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., 2nd c.BC)}}; τὴν Πιερίδα ταῖς θεαῖς{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.2.25; θέατρον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 52; ἱερά “Anthologia Graeca” 9.340.}}
**II.3** ἀ. πόλεμον **declare** war, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“SIG” 742.12 (Ephesus, 1st c.BC).}}

323:
** ἀνά-δειξις**, εως, ἡ,
**showing forth:** especially **public proclamation** or **appointment** to an office, ἡ τῶν ὑπάτων ἀ.{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 8; τῶν συναρχόντων ἡ ἀναγόρευσις καὶ ἀ. “CG” 12: _absolutely_, ἡ ἀ.}}**the election, Cat.Mi.** {{Refs|44, 46.}}
**2** ἡ ἀ τοῦ διαδήματος **ceremony** of coronation, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.25.11 (_plural_)}}; **dedication** of temple, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.6.23.}}
**3** **declaration,** χρόνων{{LXX|LXX.Sir.43.6.}}
**II** (from _passive_) **manifestation,** of Osiris, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.85}}, but rather from _active_ in ἡ ἀ. αὐτοῦ πρὸς τὸν Ἰσραήλ{{NT|NT.Luke.1.80.}}

324:
** ἀναδέχομαι**,
_future_ -δέξομαι: _aorist_ ἀνεδεξάμην, _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ ἀνεδέγμην (see. below): _perfect_ _passive_ ἀναδέδεγμαι:— **take up, catch, receive,** σάκος δ᾽ ἀνεδέξατο πολλά (i.e. δόρατα) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.619; ἀ. πληγὰς εἰς τὸ σῶμα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 4; βέλη τῷ σώματι “Marc.” 10.}}
**2** **receive, entertain** as a guest, {{NT|NT.Act.28.7.}}
**II** **take upon oneself, submit to,** ἀνεδέγμεθ᾽ ὀϊζύν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.563, compare 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 60; ἁμαρτήματα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.36; πόλεμον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.88.12; ἀπέχθειαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Eumenes” 6; ἀ. τι ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτόν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.64, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.3}}: _absolutely_, **acknowledge** one's evidence, of an absent witness, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 46.7.}}
**II.2** **accept, receive,** ἀγγελίαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.41 (+others -δείξατ᾽)}}; λουτρὰ.. μητρὸς ἀνεδέξω πάρα {{Refs|E “IT” 818}}; χορηγίας, ἡγεμονίαν, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aristides” 1, 23; τὸν κλῆρον “Cic.” 43; τῶν σωμάτων τὰ μανὰ ἀ. θερμότητα “Cat.Mi.” 61 (uncertain)}}; **accept** a statement, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “in Aristotelis Physica paraphrasis - pars” 77.8.}}
**II.3** **admit of,** κλίσιν, ἀριθμόν{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 29.9, +others; σχέσιν πρός τι 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 122.}}
**II.4** **undertake** to say or do, with _future_ _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.91, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.17, etc.}}: with _aorist_ _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aristides” 14.}}
**II.4.b** **undertake,** with _accusative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 157; ὅσα ὑπισχνεῖτο καὶ ἀνεδέχετο 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.7}}; **take upon oneself,** αἰτίαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 365d}}; πρεσβείας, κινδύνους, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 339.20 (Sestos, 2nd c.BC), 441.9 (Stratonicea, 1st c.BC).}}
**II.5** **give security to one,** τινί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.81; τινί τι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.25.9}}; **go bail for,** τινά{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 12.4; τινὰ τῶν χρημάτων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.16.8}}; ἀ. τοὺς δανειστάς **undertake to satisfy** them, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 11}}; ἀ. τὴν πίστιν ὑπέρ τινος{{Refs|[prev. author] “Phoc.” 14: _absolutely_, “Leg.Gort.” 9.24,41.}}
**II.6** **take back,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.58.}}
**II.7** **experience, suffer,** πάθος, ταραχάς, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.82 W., “D.” 1.13; σῆψιν 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 13.3.}}
**III** **wait for,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.52.8.}}

325:
** ἀναδίδωμι**,
_poetry_ ἀνδ-: _future_ -δώσω, etc.:— **give up, hold up and give,** φιάλαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).39, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.8.}}
**2** **deliver,** ἐπιστολάς{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 29.10.7, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.45, compare “IG” 14.830; ψήφισμα “OGI” 437.78 (Pergam., 1st c.BC).}}
**II** **give forth, send up,** especially of the earth, **yield,** καρπόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 15, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 12, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 484.4; ὡραῖα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58.}}
**II.2** **send up,** Φερσεφόνα.. ἀνδιδοῖ ψυχὰς πάλιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 133.3.}}
**II.3** of a river, ἀ. θρόμβους ἀσφάλτου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.179}}; of a volcano, ὰ. πῦρ καὶ καπνόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.88, etc.; ἀ. εὐωδίαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.645f, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sudore” 10.}}
**II.4** _intransitive_, of springs, fire, etc., **burst, issue forth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.26, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 351a15}} (also _passive_, τὰ ἐν ἄντροις ἀναδιδόμενα ὕδατα{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Antro Nympharum - Porphyrii Opuscula” 6).}}
**II.5** **send up to** higher authority, **present** by name, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFay.” 26.13 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**II.5.b** in Mathematics texts, in _passive_, **to be given,** of elements in calculation, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 21.1.}}
**III** **deal round, distribute, impart,** διαβούλιον τοῖς φίλοις{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.58.2}}; of one person, τὴν πρᾶξίν τινι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|8.17.2; τοῖς λόχοις τὰς ψήφους 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 10.57, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 11, etc.}}; ἀ. φήμην **spread** it, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aem.” 25}}:—_passive_, ἀνεδίδοντο χρυσοῖ στέφανοι{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 17.}}
**III.2** in Medicine texts, **distribute** food, juices, etc., throughout the body, Philotim. cited in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 2.69.9, +others}}: especially in _passive_, Dieuch.{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 4.7.1, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.14; πέττεσθαί τε καὶ ἀναδίδοσθαι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.457, compare 6.650, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 1.47.}}
**IV** _middle_, **sell,** [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 558 (probably _falsa lectio_ for{ἀποδόσθαι}).}}]
**V** in Grammars, ἀ. τὸν τόνον **throw back** accent, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Etymologicum Magnum” 739.22, Scholia Ven. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.182.}}
**VI** _intransitive_, **go backwards, retrograde** (compare ἐπιδίδωμι), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1390b28.}}

327:
** ἀναζητ-έω**,
**investigate,** τὰς αἰτίας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 693a; τὰ ὑπὸ γῆς “Ap.” 18b}}: —_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.137, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 26, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.8.}}
**II** **search out, discover,** τὰ παραλελειμμένα ὑπὸ τῶν προτέρων ἀρχείων{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 267 (Pergam., 3rd c.BC)}}; **search for,** μαστροπούς{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.40.}}

328:
** ἀναζώννῡμι**
or ἀναζωννύω, _future_ -ζώσω, **gird up again, recall to service,** _metaphorically_, τινὰ ἐπὶ τοὺς λόγους{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 18.224a; τὸ ἕκτον βιβλίον πρὸς τὴν ἀϊδίαν κίνησιν 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 1118.6:}} —_middle_, ἀ. τὰς ὀσφύας **gird** up one's loins, {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.1Pet.1.13; ἀ. πέπλους 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 19.73}}; ἀνεζωσμένοι, Latin _alte praecincti,_ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Polycrates Historicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.139d}}:—_passive_, **to be held in check,** of passions, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.117.}}
**2** _middle_, with _accusative_, πόλεμον **embark on,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis Epiph. p.361 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}

329:
** ἀναζωπῠρ-έω**,
**rekindle, light up again,** in tmesi, ἀν᾽ αὖ σὺ ζωπυρεῖς νείκη νέα{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1121; θερμῷ τὸ θερμὸν ἀ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Spiritu” 484a7; τὴν ὅλην φύσιν 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 4.151c; τὸ χάρισμα τοῦ θεοῦ NT.2Tim.1.6}}:—_passive_, **to be rekindled,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 527d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.46 (_metaphorically_)}}; **to be excited,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 25.112.}}
**II** _intransitive_ in _active_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 41, etc.}}

330:
** ἀναθάλλω**,
_aorist_ ἀνέθηλα{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 5.4, “NA” 2.25}}: _aorist 2_ ἀνέθαλον{{NT|NT.Phil.4.10}}:—**shoot up again, sprout afresh,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus}} [same places]:—_future_ _passive_ in _active_ sense, ἀναθαλήσεται στάχυς{{LXX|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.281 (Heraclas Medicus): _metaphorically_, ἡ σὰρξ ἀ. LXX.Psa.27.7.}}
**II** _transitive_, **make to flourish, revive,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Sir.11.22, 50.10, NT.Phil.[same place]}}

331:
** ἀνάθεμα**,
_poetry_ ἄνθεμα, ατος, τό, (ἀνατίθημι) properly, like{ἀνάθημα}, **anything dedicated,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Epigrammata” 13.2, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.162 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2693d (from Mylasa), +others, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.85 K.}}
**2** **anything devoted to evil, an accursed thing,** {{LXX|LXX.Lev.27.28, LXX.Deu.7.26, 13.17, +others}}; of persons. {{NT|NT.Rom.9.3, NT.1Cor.12.3, etc.}}
**II** **curse,** [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|Tab.Defix.Aud. 41 B (Megara, 1st-2nd c.AD), compare {ἀναθεματιαῖος}}}]

332:
** ἀναθεμᾰ-τίζω**,
**devote to evil,** {{LXX|LXX.Num.21.2, LXX.Josh.6.20, +others, “Tab.Defix. Aud.” 41 A, “Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 1.1.5.3; ἀναθέματι ἀ. LXX.Deu.13.15}}; but ἀναθέματι ἀ. ἑαυτούς **bind** themselves **by a curse,** with _infinitive_, {{NT|NT.Act.23.14}}:—_passive_, **to be devoted to evil,** {{LXX|LXX.Num.18.14.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **curse and swear,** {{NT|NT.Mark.14.71.}}

334:
** ἀνάθημα**, ατος, τό,
(ἀνατίθημι) **that which is set up:** hence, like{ἄγαλμα}, **votive offering set up** in a temple, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.14, 92, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 286, etc.; ἀ. ἐκ λειτουργιῶν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 26.4.}}
**2** used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in first sense of ἄγαλμα}} **delight, ornament,** μολπή τ᾽ ὀρχηστύς τε· τὰ γάρ τ᾽ ἀναθήματα δαιτός{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.152, compare 21.430, “IG” 14.1390}}; τοῖς τεκοῦσιν ἀνάθημα βιότου, of children, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 518, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 364b}}; to help deserving poverty is βασιλικοῦ πλούτου ἀ. καὶ κατασκεύασμα λαμπρότατον{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 19.14.}}
**3** of a slave in a temple, ἀ. πόλεως **devoted to** this service by the city, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 310.}} —Cf. ἀνάθεμα.

335:
** ἀναίδεια**,
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ἀναιδεία; _Attic dialect_ also ἀναιδείᾱ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 226}}, _poetry_ ἀναιδία {{Refs|Hdn.Gr. 2.453}}:— **shamelessness,** ἀναιδείην ἐπιειμένε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.149; ἀναιδείης ἐπιβῆναι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.424; ἡ γαστὴρ φρένας παρήγαγεν εἰς ἀναιδείην 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 78; ἀναιδείῃ διαχρεώμενοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.210, compare 6.129; ἀναιδείας πλέα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 607}}; μετ᾽ ἀναιδείας, ={ἀναιδῶς}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254d; εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ἧκεν ἀναιδείας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.22.}}
**II** in the Areopagus, λίθος ἀναιδείας was the stone of **unforgivingness,** on which stood an accuser who demanded the full penalty of the law against one accused of homicide (see. αἰδέομαι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|11.3), 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.28.5}}; compare ὕβρις.

336:
** ἀναίρ-εσις**, εως, ἡ,
**taking up** or **away,** especially of dead bodies for burial, ὀστέων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 404; νεκρῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.109, 113; οἳ ἂν μὴ εὑρεθῶσιν ἐς ἀναίρεσιν 2.34, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.68, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.7; ἀναίρεσιν δοῦναι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 18}}; in a sea-fight, νεκρῶν ἢ ναυαγίων ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.72; τῶν ναυαγῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.5.}}
**2** **taking up,** ἀ. καὶ θέσις ὅπλων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 814a, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.6.}}
**3** **undertaking,** ἔργων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 847b.}}
**II** **destruction,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.5; τειχῶν καὶ πόλεων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.141; φθορὰ ἡ κατ᾽ ἀναίρεσιν “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.266.}}
**II.2** **slaying, putting to death,** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Num.11.15, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 5.2.11, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1051d}}; **banishment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ εὐσχημοσύνης” 2.}}
**II.3** **repeal,** δογμάτων{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 34}}; **quashing** of indictment, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ τῶν στάσεων” 3.}}
**II.4** **direct confutation** of arguments, opposed to διαίρεσις (confutation by drawing a distinction), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 183a10}}; **destruction** (by argument), τινός{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 12.}}
**II.5** Astrology texts, ={ἀκτινοβολία}, Thrasyll. cited in {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “in Ptolemaei Tetrabiblon” 189.}}

337:
** ἀναιρέω**,
_perfect_ ἀνῄρηκα (ἀνειρ-uncertain in {{Refs|“Comica Adespota” 18.6D.): (see. αἱρέω)}}: — **take up,** ἀνελόντες ἀπὸ χθονός **having raised** the victim from the ground, so as to cut its throat (compare αὐερύω), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.453.}}
**2** **take up and carry off, bear away,** especially prizes, ἀέθλια{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.736, compare 551; στεφανηφόρους ἀγῶνας ἀναραιρηκότα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.102; Ὀλύμπια ἀναραιρηκώς 6.36, compare 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.1.}}
**3** simply, **take up,** παῖδα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.61; τὰ ὀστᾶ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126.}}
**4** **take up** bodies for burial, ἀνελόντες καὶ κατακλαύσαντες{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 386, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.9}}; more common in _middle_, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.3.}}
**II** **make away with, destroy,** of men, **kill,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.66; πολλοὺς ἀναιρῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 990}}; σὲ μὲν ἡμετέρα ψῆφος ἀ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 517; θανάτοις ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 870d; ἐκ πολιτείας τοιαῦτα θηρία ἀ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 3.19, etc.}}
**II.2** of things, **abrogate, annul,** ὅρους ἀνεῖλον πολλαχῇ πεπηγότας{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 36.4; νόμον 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.39; διαθήκας 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.14; στήλας 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.103; ἀταξίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.35, etc.; ἐκ μέσου ἀ. βλασφημίας [prev. author] 10.36; τηλικαύτην ἀνελόντας μαρτυρίαν [prev. author] 28.5}}; **abolish,** τὰς τῶν παρανόμων γραφάς{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 29.4}}:— _passive_, ἀνῄρηνται ὀλιγαρχίαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.1.}}
**II.3** **destroy** an argument, **confute** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}}; especially **confute directly,** opposed to διαιρέω (see. ἀναίρεσις 11.4){{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 176b36, +others}}; ἀ. ἑαυτὸν **confute** oneself, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 25.14.}}
**II.4** in argument, **do away with,** τὰς ὑποθέσεις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 533c}}; **deny,** opposed to τιθέναι, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.192, +others}}
**III** **appoint, ordain,** of oracle's answer to inquiry, ὁ θεὸς αὐτοῖς ἀ. παραδοῦναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.25; οὓς ἂν ὁ θεὸς ἀνέλῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 865d, compare 642d; ἀνεῖλεν θεοῖς οἷς ἔδει θύειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.6}}: also with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ἢν τὸ χρηστήριον ἀνέλῃ μιν βασιλέα εἶναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.13, etc.}}: _absolutely_, **answer, give a response,** ἀνεῖλε τὸ χρηστήριον {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}; ἀ. τι περί τινος **give an oracle** about a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 914a; μαντείας ἀ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 1.16}}:—_passive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] 21.51.}}
**B** _middle_, **take up for oneself, take up, pick up,** οὐλοχύτας ἀνέλοντο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.449; ἀσπίδα, ἔγχος, 11.32, 13.296; κυνέην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84; δίκτυα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 602b9}}; **achieve, win,** ἀ. τὴν Ὀλυμπιάδα, τὴν νίκην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.70, 103, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.47}}; generally, ἀ. ἐπιφροσύνας **take** thought, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.22; εὐδαιμονίαν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.56, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 281}}; in bad sense, ὄνειδος σπαργάνων ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1035}}; εἴ σ᾽ ἀνελοίμην if **I should take** thee **into my service,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.357}}; σῖτα ἀ. **get** forage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.128}}; ποινὴν τῆς Αἰσώπου ψυχῆς ἀ. **exact** vengeance for.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.134.}}
**B.2** **take up and carry off, snatch,** κούρας ἀνέλοντο θύελλαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.66; ἀναιρούμενος οἴκαδε φέρειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 914b}}; ἀνείλατο (for the form{{Refs 5th c.AD+|compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus) δαίμων “epigram Gr.” 404.1.}}
**B.3** **take up for burial** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.4), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.14, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.97, etc.; πατέρων ἀρίστων σώμαθ᾽ ὧν ἀνειλόμην 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1167; τὰ ὀστέα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.41}}; of the ashes of the dead, πυρὸς ἀ. ἄθλιον βάρος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1140}}; of one still living, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1616, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.13; τοὺς ναυαγούς [prev. work] 1.7.4, compare 11; τοὺς δέκα στρατηγοὺς τοὺς οὐκ ἀνελομένους τοὺς ἐκ τῆς ναυμαχίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 32b}}:—_passive_, ἀναιρεθέντων τῶν νεκρῶν.. ὑγιὴς ἀνῃρέθη{{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 614b, +others}}
**B.4** **take up in one's arms,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.8}}: hence, **take up** new-born children, **own** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 36, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 531}}; **take up** an exposed child, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 159, compare “BGU” 1110, etc.}}
**B.5** **conceive in the womb,** with _accusative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.108, 6.69.}}
**B.6** **take up** money at interest, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.17.}}
**B.7** **take up** a lease, {{Refs|“Michel” 1359 (from Chios). compare “BCH”_7.204.}}
**B.8** **withdraw** money from a bank, etc., αὐτὸς ἀνελέσθω{{Refs|“IG” 5(2).159.}}
**B.II** **take** **upon oneself, undertake,** πόνους{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.108}}; πόλεμόν τινι war **against** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.36; πολέμους ἀναιρούμεσθα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 492, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.7; ἀ. ἔχθραν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 233c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.20}}; ἀ. δημόσιον ἔργον **undertake, contract for the execution of** a work, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 921d, compare a, b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.21.}}
**B.II.2** **accept as one's own, adopt,** γνώμην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.16. ; τὰ οὐνόματα τὰ ἀπὸ τῶν βαρβάρων ἥκοντα 2.52}}; ἀ. φιλοψυχίην **entertain** a love for life, {{Refs|6.29.}}
**B.III** **rescind, cancel,** συγγραφήν, συνθήκας, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.31, 48.46, “IG” 7.3171 (Orchom. _Boeotian dialect_, 3rd c.BC).}}

338:
** ἀναίτιος**, ον,
also α, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.110, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 873}}:—in the best authors, only of persons, **not being the fault** or **cause of** a thing, **guiltless,** ἀναίτιον αἰτιάασθαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.775, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.135, etc.; αἰτία ἑλομένου, θεὸς ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 617e}}; ἀναίτιος ἀθανάτοις **guiltless** before the gods, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 827, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 730; ἀ. παρά τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.10; ἀ. αἷμα ἐκχέαι “SIG” 1181.6.}}
**2** with _genitive_ _of things_, **guiltless of** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.129, 7.233, etc.}}; φόνου, κακῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1505, “Ch.” 873; κακίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 42d; ἀφροσύνης 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1. 5.10: οὐκ ἀναίτιόν ἐστι}} with _infinitive_, **it is blamable** to do, {{Refs|[prev. work] 5.5.22.}}
**II** **not being the cause,** τὸ ἀ. τιθέναι ὡς αἴτιον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 65b16, compare “Rh.” 1401b30}}; **having no cause, unjustifiable,** κολάσεις{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.52 W.}}_adverb_ -ως **not in the form of a cause,** ἀ. τὴν αἰτίαν ἔχειν{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.7.2}}; **without assigning any reason,** ὁλοσχερῶς καὶ ἀ. λεκτέον{{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis de Caelo commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 665.11.}}
**III** **uncaused.** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.1.1, 6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 277.1}}: _superlative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 672}}. _adverb_ **-ως** **without a cause,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.36, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 3.67, 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 641.10; ἀ. γίγνεσθαι 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Metaphysica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 309.15.}}

339:
** ἀνακᾰθίζω**,
**set up:** whence _middle_, **sit up,** εἰς τὴν κλίνην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 60b.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **sit up,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 3, 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 27.8}}; δὶς ἑπτὰ[μησὶν] -ει [τὰ βρέφη] {{Refs|“Theologumena Arithmeticae” 48}}; of a hare listening, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.7.}}

343:
** ἀνακᾰλύπτω**,
_Doric dialect_ ἀγκ-, **uncover,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 4.952.62 (Epid.)}}; **reveal,** τι πρός τινα{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4 85.6}}; τινά, i. e. his character, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Philochorus Historicus 20}}; ἀ. λόγους **use open** speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1146; ἀ. κάρα}}**unveil oneself, Or.** {{Refs|294}}: so in _middle_, **unveil oneself,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.6.}}
**II** **remove a covering,** βλεφάρων μὴ ἀνακαλυφθέντων{{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Sensu” 444b25, cf. NT.2Cor.3.14.}}

344:
** ἀνακάμπ-τω**,
**bend convexly,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 385b33 (_passive_)}}; **bend back,** τῷ δ᾽ οὐ πάλιν θυμὸς ἀνεκάμπτετ᾽{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 16.82.}}
**II** **make to return,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 12.}}
**II.2** mostly _intransitive_, **bend back, return,** ταύτῃ λῆγον ἀ. ἐς τὰ εἴρηται τὸ ὄρος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.8; ἡ περιφορὰ ἐπ᾽ ἀρχὴν ἀ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 407a30, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 72b; πάλιν ἀ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 337a6, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 341, etc.}}
**II.2.b** **walk up and down,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.4.16, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.796d, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.139.}}
**II.2.c** in Logic, of the terms of a proposition, **to be converted,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 72b36, “de An.” 407a28.}}
**II.2.d** ἀνακάμπτων, name of **a throw of the dice,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 57.}}

345:
** ἀνάκειμαι**,
_poetry_ ἄγκ-, serving as _passive_ to ἀνατίθημι, **to be laid up** as a votive offering in the temple, **to be dedicated,** κρητῆρές οἱ.. ἓξ χρύσεοι ἀνακέαται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.14; ἀ. ἐν ἱρῷ [prev. author] 2.135; πρὸς τοῖς ἱεροῖς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.28: _metaphorically_, αἶνος Ὀλυμπιονίκαις ἄγκειται 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11(10).8, compare 13.36}}; λόγος τῷ θεῷ ἀ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 197e; ἐν οὐρανῷ παράδειγμα ἀ “R.” 592b.}}
**b** **to be set up** as a statue in public, Σόλων ἀνάκειται παράδειγμα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.251, compare “IG” 14.1389i8; χρύσεοί κ᾽ ἀνεκείμεθα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.33, compare 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 51.}}
**2** **to be ascribed** or **offered,** αἱ πράξεις ἀ. τινι{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 1; ἡ ἡγεμονία ἀ. τινι [prev. author] “Arist.” 15; ἐς τοὺς ἀστέρας τοὺς ἑπτὰ.. τὰς ἡμέρας ἀνακεῖσθαι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.18, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Polemo Sophista “Cynegirus” 15.}}
**II** πᾶν or πάντα ἀνάκειται ἔς τινα everything **is referred to** a person, **depends on** his will, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97, 3.31: so with _dative_ _person_, πάντων ἀνακειμένων τοῖς Ἀθηναίοις ἐς τὰς ναῦς}} since **they had** their whole fortunes **depending** on their ships, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.71; ἐπὶ σοὶ τάδε πάντ᾽ ἀνάκειται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 638; ἅπαντα.. ἐπὶ τῇ τύχῃ μᾶλλον ἀ. ἢ τῇ προνοίᾳ 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.6}}; of persons, σοὶ ἀνακείμεσθα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 934}}; εἰς θάνατον ἦν ἀνακείμενα τοῖς ἀλογήσασι the death penalty **was reserved** for.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.6.5; λιμὸς εἰς ὑστάτην ἀνακείμενος ἀναισχυντίαν 18.1.1.}}
**II.2** **to be put aside,** ταῦτα ἀνακείσθω{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “in Aristotelis Physica paraphrasis - pars” 29.20.}}
**III** **lie at table, recline,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 756, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philippides Comicus 30, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 6b12, “fragment” 607, 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 40 Mein. (om. Kock), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 13.6.8, NT.Matt.9.10, +others; compare 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 191.}}

347:
** ἀνα-κλίνω**,
_poetry_ ἀγκλ-, (see. κλίνω) **lean** one thing **upon** another, [τόξον] ποτὶ γαίῃ ἀγκλίνας **having laid** it on the ground, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.113; Ἔρως ἀνακλίνας τοῦ τόξου τὸν πῆχυν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.1}}; ἀ. ἑαυτοὺς ἐπὶ τὸ ἐναντίον, of sailors struggling against the wind, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mechanica” 851b13}}; **cause to recline** at table, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.4.5, NT.Luke.12.37}}:—mostly in _passive_, **lie, sink,** or **lean back, recline,** ἀνακλινθεὶς πέσεν ὕπτιος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.371}}; of persons asleep, {{Refs|18.189}}; of rowers, {{Refs|13.78}}; of the elephant, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 498a11}}; to be **strung,** of strings of lyre, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.10.}}
**2** _passive_, of ground, **lie sloping upwards,** {{Refs|Gp. 2.3.1.}}
**II** **push** or **put back,** and so, **open,** θύρην ἀγκλίνας{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.156}}; so of the door of Olympus, ἠμὲν ἀνακλῖναι πυκινὸν νέφος ἠδ᾽ ἐπιθεῖναι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.751, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Apollinem” 6}}; τὴν θύρην τὴν καταπηκτὴν ἀ., i. e. the trap-door, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.16.}}
**III** **throw** the head **back,** and so, **lift up,** τὴν τῆς ψυχῆς αὐγήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 540a.}}
**IV** **overthrow,** of earthquake, compared to batteringram, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 7.24.10.}}

348:
** ἀνα-κόπτω**,
**drive back, push back,** θυρέων δ᾽ ἀνέκοπτεν ὀχῆας{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.47.}}
**2** **beat back** an assailant, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.12, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 38.}}
**3** ἀ. ναῦν **check** a ship's course, variant in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 25.2.}}
**4** **return** food, διὰ ῥινῶν, εἰς τὰς ῥῖνας, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus in “Rh.Mus.” 58.86, 90 and 96, compare 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 1.6.}}
**II** **knock out,** τὰς ὄψεις ἀνακοπείς{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Her.Prooem.” 2.}}
**II.2** **cut from below,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 9.18.}}
**II.3** **beat up** eggs, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.222, “PMag.Lond.” 121.180.}}
**III** **check, stop,** ἦχον{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 22; προσδοκίαν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 25; ἀοιδήν 5th-6th c.AD: Coluthus Epicus 125}}:—_passive_, **to be stopped, restrained,** τῆς ὁρμῆς{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 57, compare “PFlor.” 36.3}}; **stop short** in a speech, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 35.}}
**IV** in Medicine texts, **take effect,** ἀνακόπτει γὰρ οὕτως ἡ ὠφέλεια{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus in “Rh.Mus.” 58.92 (perhaps διακ.,compare “SIG” 1170.16).}}

349:
** ἀνακράζω**,
_future_ -κράξομαι or _future_ _perfect_ **-κεκράξομαι**{{LXX|LXX.Joel.3(4).16}}: _aorist_ ἀνέκρᾰγο; late ἀνέκραξα{{LXX+NT|LXX.Judg.7.20, “BGU” 1201.11, NT.Mark.1.23, +others}}:—**cry out, lift up the voice, shout,** ἐπεὶ.. ἀνέκραγον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.467}}; εἴ τι πέραν ἀερθεὶς ἀνέκραγον if I **raised my voice** too high, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.76; ἐξ ἑνὸς στόματος ἅπαντες ἀνέκραγον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 670, compare “V.” 1311, etc.}}; οὐκ ἀνέκραγεν, of a dying man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.44; πρῶτος ἐπὶ τοῦ βήματος ἀνέκραγεν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 28.3}}: followed by a _relative_, ἀνέκραγον ὡς εὖ λέγοι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 431, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.1.14}}; τηλικαῦτ᾽ ἀνεκράγετε, ὡς.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.215: with}}_infinitive_, ἀνακραγόντων βάλλειν.. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 34.}}
**2** rarely of animals, ἂν γλαὺξ ἀνακράγῃ{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 534.11.}}

350:
** ἀνακρίνω**,
**examine closely, interrogate,** especially judicially, Παυσανίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.95, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 201e; ἀ. τινὰ πόθεν ζῇ 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 32.3}}; **sound** a person, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.20.12.}}
**2** **inquire into,** ἀ. τοὺς ἐργασαμένους{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.2}}; τὴν [αἰτίαν] {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 994 “fragment” 21, compare “Lib.” p.21 O.:}} —_middle_, ἀ. ποινὰ τίς ἔσται what remedy there shall be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.63.}}
**II** **examine** magistrates so as to prove their qualification, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.66 and 70.}}
**II.2** of the magistrates, **examine** persons concerned in a suit, so as to prepare the matter for trial, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.101, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.32}}; ὁ ἄρχων ἀνέκρινε πᾶσιν ὑμῖν καὶ ἀνακρίνας εἰσήγαγεν εἰς τὸ δικαστήριον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.31, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 56.6}}:—_passive_, ἀνεκρίθησαν αἱ ἀμφισβητήσεις{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.23:}} —_middle_, οὐκ ἀνεκρίνατο ταύτην [τὴν γραφήν] **he did** not **have** it **examined,** of the prosecutor, {{Refs|[prev. author] 21.103, compare 53.17.}}
**II.3** generally, **examine,** μάρτυρας{{Refs|“SIG” 953.46 (from Calymna)}}; τινά 1{{Refs|“Cor.” 9.3}}:—_middle_, {{Refs|“Michel” 409.9 (from Cos).}}
**II.4** **select,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Pseudo-Callisthenes Historicus 3.26.}}
**III** in _middle_, _absolutely_, ἀνακρίνεσθαι πρὸς ἑωυτούς **dispute, wrangle** one with another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.56.}}

351:
** ἀνάκρισις**,
_poetry_ ἄγκρ-, εως, ἡ, (ἀνακρίνω{{Refs|11.1)}} **examination** of the qualifications of magistrates, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.85.}}
**II** (ἀνακρίνω{{Refs|11.2)}} **previous examination** of parties concerned in a suit, **preparation** of the matter for trial, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 5.2, etc., compare “PSI” 4.392 (3rd c.BC), “OGI” 374 (_plural_), NT.Act.25.26}}; of the magistrate, ἀνάκρισιν διδόναι, παραδιδόναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 176c, “Lg.” 855e}}; of the parties, εἰς ἀνάκρισιν ἥκειν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.13, etc.}}; μηδ᾽ εἰς ἄγκρισιν ἐλθεῖν, i. e. should not even **begin proceedings** (where however the {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Scholia explains ἐς ἄγκρισιν by ἐς μάχην,compare ἀνακρίνω 111), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 364}}; οὐδ᾽ ἀ. μοι δώσεις you will not allow me **the first forms of law,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Chrm.” [prev. cited]}}
**III** generally, **inquiry, examination,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 277e.}}
**IV** **preliminary examination** of a slave before sale, {{Refs|“POxy.” 1463.12, etc.}}
**V** **examination, testing** of magical ingredients, etc., {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.1992, 2007.}}
**VI** **quarrel, dispute,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.69}}; **disputation,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.72 M.}}

352:
** ἀνακύπτω**,
_future_ **-κύψομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 146, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 302a}} **-ψω** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMar.” 3.1}}: _aorist_ ἀνέκυψα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.91, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἀνακέκῡφα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 212, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 7.10}}:—**lift up the head,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 11.3}}; ἀνακεκυφώς **with the head high,** of a horse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus}} [same place]; κἀγκύψας (for καὶ ἀνακύψας) ἔχε and keep **your head up,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 236; ἐν ὀροφῇ ποικίλματα θεώμενος ἀνακύπτων}}**throwing his head back,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 529b; ἀνακύψατε καὶ ἐπάρατε τὰς κεφαλάς NT.Luke.21.28; especially in drinking, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 613a13, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus [same place]; ἐπικύπτειν καὶ ἀ. 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.146.}}
**II** **come up out of the water, pop up,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1063; ἐκ τῆς θαλάσσης εἰς τὸν ἐνθάδε τόπον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 109d}}; ἀ. μέχρι τοῦ αὐχένος, opposed to καταδῦναι, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 171d, compare “Phdr.” 249c.}}
**II.b** _metaphorically_, **emerge, crop up,** ὅτι ἐξ αὐτῶν καλόν τι ἀνακύψοιτο{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Euthd.” 302a; αἱ -κύπτουσαι χρεῖαι 1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 11.7, compare 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.25e, “Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 1.2.17.}}
**II.c** of persons, **rise out of difficulties, breathe again,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.91, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 11.5; τὰ τῶν Καρχηδονίων ἀνέκυψε 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.55.1, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 13.35; ἀπὸ τῶν μυχῶν τοῦ σώματος 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 6.}}

353:
** ἀναλαμβάνω**,
_future_ -λήψομαι: _Ionic dialect_ _perfect_ _infinitive_ _passive_ -λελάμφθαι or **-λελάφθαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 11}}, _participle_ **-λελαμμένος**{{Refs|[prev. author] “Art.” 11}}:—**take up, take into one's hands,** τὸ παιδίον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.111; τὰ τόξα, τὰ ὅπλα, etc., 3.78, 9.46}}; **take on board ship,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.166, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.25, etc.}}; **take up into heaven,** in _passive_, {{LXX+NT|LXX.4Ki.2.9, NT.Act.1.11}}: and generally, **take with one,** especially of troops, supplies, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.51, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.64, 8.27, etc.}}; _participle_ ἀναλαβών often = **with,** ἄνδρας ἀναλαβὼν ἡγήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.36, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.7.}}
**b** **suspend** in a sling, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 22.}}
**c** _metaphorically_, **take up,** for the purpose of examining, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22b, “Men.” 87e, +others}}
**2** **receive,** φιλοφρόνως ἀ.{{Refs|[prev. author] “Ep.” 329d, etc.}}; of women, ἀ. τὴν γονήν **conceive,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.495e.}}
**3** **take upon oneself, assume,** τὴν προξενίαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.89; τὴν ἀρχήν “Inscription Prien.” 123}}; κόσμον, of a king, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 383.135; ἐσθῆτα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aristides” 21}}; πρόσωπον, σχῆμα, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 11, “Somn.” 13.}}
**4** in _middle_, **undertake, engage in,** ἀναλαβέσθαι κίνδυνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.69:}} —also _active_, ἀντὶ τῆς φιλίας τὸν πόλεμον ἀναλαβεῖν Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.78.}}
**5** **take up, adopt,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 76}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.52, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 172.}}
**6** of money, **confiscate,** in _passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 338.24 (Pergam.), “PSI” 1.104.10 (late 2nd c.AD)}}; κλῆρος ἀνειλημμένος εἰς τὸ βασιλικόν{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 61b74 (2nd c.BC), etc., 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.484a, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.181.}}
**7** **learn by rote,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.16.5, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 20, 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Topicorum libros octo commentaria - pars” 494.31.}}
**8** **include,** τῷ νόμῳ{{Refs|“OGI” 629 (from Palmyra).}}
**9** in Medicine texts, **make up** ingredients, κηρῷ καὶ νάρδῳ{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.1, compare 2.3 (_passive_)}}; so in Magic, ἀ. οἴνῳ καὶ μέλιτι, ὄξει, {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.1316, 2690.}}
**10** **raise, erect** a wall, {{Refs|“IG” 2.1054.9.}}
**11** _absolutely_, **lift up** one's voice, {{LXX|LXX.Num.14.1.}}
**II** **get back, regain, recover,** τὴν ἀρχήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.73, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.10; ἀ. ἐπιστήμην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 85d; οὐδ᾽ ἀφέντι λίθον δυνατὸν ἀναλαβεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1114a18.}}
**II.2** **retrieve, make good,** τὴν αἰτίην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.231; ἁμαρτίαν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1249, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 426; τὴν ἀρχαίαν ἀρετήν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.14; ταῦτα ἀ. καὶ μεταγιγνώσκειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.109.}}
**II.3** **restore, repair,** τὴν προτέρην κακότητα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.109: _absolutely_, [prev. author] 5.121; ἀ. τὴν πόλιν ἐκ τῆς πρόσθεν ἀθυμίας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.21}}; ἀ. ἑαυτόν **recover** oneself, **regain strength,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.26, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 10D., 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 243}}; **collect** oneself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.22: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 467b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.163, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.118.}}
**II.4** **take up again, resume,** in narrative or argument, τὸν λόγον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.62, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 544b, +others; πολλάκις ἀ. [prev. author] “Phd.” 95e}}; ἀναλαβεῖν διεξιόντα **repeat** in detail, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Euthd.” 275c}}; at Rome, ἀ. θυσίας, = **instaurare sacra,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 25; ἀ. τῇ μνήμῃ}}**recollect,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 294d}}; without τῇ μνήμῃ, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 21}}; but ἀ. μνήμην **recover** a memory, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Memoria” 451a22}}; πρὸς ἑαυτὸν ἀ. **run over** in one's mind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 26a.}}
**II.5** **receive back** into a family, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Abd.” 9, +others}}
**III** **pull up short,** of a horse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 3.5}}; **check,** οἷόνπερ ἵππον τὸν λόγον ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 701c; τὴν ὁρμὴν τῆς νεώς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.3.4}}; ἀ. τὰς κύνας **call** them **back,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 7.10.}}
**IV** **win over,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 682, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.28, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 24, +others; ἀ. τὸν ἀκροατήν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1354b32.}}
**V** ἀγορὰν στοαῖς ἀνειλημμένην διτταῖς, perhaps on a terrace **supported** by two colonnades, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Dicaearchus Geographus 59.23; compare ἀνάλημμα 11.}}

354:
** ἀνά-ληψις**,
later -λημψις, εως, ἡ, hyperdorian mode -λαψις Ti _Locrian dialect_ 100c:— **taking up,** e.g. **suspension** in a sling, **fixing** a bandage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 22, “Off.” 9}}; **looping** or **tying up** of vines, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1692.20 (2nd c.AD), compare 1631.13 (3rd c.AD).}}
**2** **receiving back** into a family, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Abd.” 5.}}
**3** **acquirement** of knowledge, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.31 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, +others, 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus [same place], 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.73, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.1; ἱερῶν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.351e.}}
**4** **assumption** of an office, uncertain in {{Refs|“Inscription Prien.” 123; διαδήματος “OGI” 383.102.}}
**5** _passive_, **being taken up** or **away,** {{Refs|Psalm.Solom. 4.20}}; **ascension,** {{NT|NT.Luke.9.51.}}
**6** **reception, entertainment**, {{Refs|“SIG” 888.36 (from Thrace).}}
**7** **assimilation,** τροφῆς {{Refs|Menon “Iatr.” 25.48.}}
**II** **recovery,** μνήμης{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Memoria” 451a20}}; **means of regaining,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 9.}}
**II.2** **making good, making amends,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.65}}; **refreshing** of soldiers after hard work, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.87.1, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Par.” 40; ἀνάληψιν ποιεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Demetrius Comicus Novae Comoediae 1.10}}:—**recovery** from illness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.27, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 83e.}}
**II.3** **repair,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.1.38, “PSI” 1.83.11 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.4** **repetition,** [{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor] “Rh.” p.200H.}}

355:
** ἀνᾱλίσκω**
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 337, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1131, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.48}}:—also ἀνᾱλόω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 10, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 813, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 325, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 248, 5th-6th c.BC: Araros Comicus 10, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.24, +others, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 280, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 11.1}}: _imperfect_ ἀνήλισκον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 552b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.16, ἀνάλισκον 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 3.58, ἀνάλουν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 220.2, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.45}}: _future_ ἀνᾱλώσω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 308, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 568d}}: _aorist_ ἀνήλωσα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1049, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.18, etc., ἀνάλωσα [ᾱ] 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 681 (assuming variant)}} and later: _perfect_ ἀνήλωκα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 26.3, etc., and ἀνάλωκα [ᾱ] 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64}} codices and later:—_passive_, _future_ ἀνᾱλωθήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 506, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.19, wrongly ἀνηλωθήσομαι “PRev.Laws” 51.17 (3rd c.BC), ἀνᾱλώσομαι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.129:}}_aorist_ ἀνηλώθην and ἀνᾱλώθην: _perfect_ ἀνήλωμαι and ἀνάλωμαι:—in Attic Inscrr. both forms are found in 5th C, ἀναλίσκω only from 4th C onwards. The augmented forms are sometimes wrongly used, ἀνηλοῦντι{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“POxy.” 1143 (1st c.AD), ἀνηλώσῃ “PStrassb.” 92.17 (3rd c.BC)}}; compare ἀνήλωμα: ἀνάλωσα is found at Amorgos, {{Refs|“IG” 12(7).22.16, and at Delos, [prev. work]11(2).161 A 114}}:—**use up, spend,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 381: _absolutely_, [prev. work] 248; τὰ ἀναλωθέντα ἀποδοῦναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.117}}; ἀ. εἴς τι **spend upon** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.83, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 220, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78a, “R.” 561a, +others; πρός τι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.19; ὑπὲρ φιλοτιμίας [prev. author] 18.66: with _dative_, Ἰσοκράτει ἀργύριον ἀ.}} **spend** money in paying him, {{Refs|[prev. author] 35.40}}:—_passive_, τἀνηλωμένα **the monies expended** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 18.113; τοῦτο γὰρ μόνον οὐκ ἔστι τἀνάλωμ᾽ ἀναλωθὲν λαβεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 776.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, ἀνήλωσας λόγον **hast wasted** words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1049, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 325; χρόνον καὶ πόνον ἐπί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 369e; σώματα καὶ πόνους πολέμῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64; τὴν τῶν προγόνων δόξαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 247b}}; ἀ. ὕπνον **waste time in** sleep, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.27}}; λόγῳ ἀ. τὸν χρόνον τῆς ἡμέρας **diem eximere dicendo,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 30.}}
**3** **consume,** σιτία{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 10; κρέα 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.4.10}}; of animals, in _passive_, **to be eaten,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321b}}:— _passive_, **to be expended,** εἰς τὴν πιμελήν in forming fat, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 727b1, +others}}
**II** of persons, **kill, destroy,** τοὺς ἀναλωθέντας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 570, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1174, “fragment” 892, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 681, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.65}}:—_middle_ **kill oneself,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.81}}: —_passive_, **to be consumed, perish,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 272d}}; **to be disposed of, got rid of,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 289c.}}(ἀνά, ἁλίσκομαι.)

356:
** ἀναλογ-ία**, ἡ,
(λόγος) **mathematical proportion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 31c, 32c; ἡ ἀ. ἰσότης ἐστὶ λόγων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1131a31}}; of progressions, ἀ. γεωμετρική{{Refs|[prev. work] b13}}; ἀριθμητική{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 1106a36, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 10.3}}; ἁρμονική Thrasyll. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Theo.Sm. p.85H., 2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus “Arithmetica Introductio” 2.22; κατὰ τὴν ἀ.}}**comparing the ratios,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1282b40}}; τὸ κατ᾽ ἀ. ἴσον{{Refs|[prev. work] 1301a27; ὑπὲρ τὴν ἀ. τινός}}**out of proportion,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 89.22.}}
**2** **proportion** generally, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1296b25, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 11.7, 10.}}
**II** **analogy,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 486b19, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 212, etc.}}
**II.2** especially **grammatical analogy,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Gellius, Aulus Grammaticus Latinus 2.25, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 36.23, etc.}}
**III** **relation,** ἀ. ἔχειν stand **in relation with,** πρός τι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.38O., compare p.51O.}}
**IV** **correspondence, resemblance,** ὁμοιότης ἢ ἀ. [τινί] {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sign.” 37, compare “fragment” 3}}; κατ᾽ -ίαν, opposed to διαφοράν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “D.” 1.22.}}

357:
** ἀναλογ-ίζομαι**,
**reckon up, sum up,** τὰ ὡμολογημένα{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 332d, compare “R.” 330e: _absolutely_, ἐκ τῶν προειρημένων ἀ. [prev. work] 524d; τὰ δεινά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.4, cf. NT.Heb.12.3}}; τὰ γεγονότα καὶ τὰ παρόντα πρὸς τὰ μέλλοντα ἀ. **calculate** the past and the present **in comparison with** the future, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 186a; ἀ. τι πρός τι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1320b20}}; ἐκ τούτων ἀ. **make calculations** from.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cael.” 293a33}}; **infer,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 14.4, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.13, 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus “fragment” 38.}}
**2** **calculate, consider,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.7, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.47.}}
**3** followed by a Conjunction, ἀ. ὡς.. **calculate** or **reflect** that, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.83, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.23, etc.}}; **take into account,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.5.}}
**4** **recapitulate,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Philippum” 4.}}

358:
** ἄνᾰλος**, ον,
(ἅλς A) **without salt, not salted,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 927a35, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.401.}}
**2** of salt itself, salt **which is no salt, ** NT.Mark.9.50.

359:
** ἀνάλῠ-σις**, εως, ἡ,
(ἀναλύω) **loosing, releasing,** κακῶν **from** evils, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 142 (Lyric poetry); ὅρκων 4th-3rd c.BC: Timaeus Historicus 23.}}
**2** **dissolving,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 394b17, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.915c (_plural_)}}; σώματος, of death, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 19.}}
**3** **resolution** of a problem **by the analysis of its conditions,** opposed to σύνθεσις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1112b23; especially in in Mathematics texts, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” 17, 3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 634.11, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in primum Euclidis librum commentarius” p.43 F.}}
**4** in the Logic of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}}, **reduction** of the imperfect figures into the perfect one, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“APr.” 51a18, +others, 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.7.}}
**5** **solution** of a problem, etc., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 12.}}
**II** (from _passive_) **retrogression,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.76d}}; **retirement, departure,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 19.4.1}}; **death** {{NT|compare (ἀναλύω 111), NT.2Tim.4.6.}}

360:
** ἀνα-λύω**,
**B**_Epic dialect_ ἀλλύω (ἀνλύω {{Refs|“Hymnus ad Isim” 145): (see. λύω}} for the tenses and prosody: {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has ἀλλύουσα, ἀλλύεσκε with ῡ)}}:— **unloose, undo,** of Penelope's web, νύκτας δ᾽ ἀλλύεσκεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.105; ἀλλύουσαν.. ἀγλαὸν ἱστόν [prev. work] 109, etc.; ἀνά τε πρυμνήσια λῦσαι [prev. work] 9.178, etc.}}
**B.2** **unloose, set free,** ἐμὲ δ᾽ ἐκ δεσμῶν ἀνέλυσαν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 12.200 (never in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”), compare 2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 22.4; ὀφθαλμόν, φωνάν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.90; τινὰ καταδίκης 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 5.18.}}
**B.3** in Medicine texts, **relax,** in _passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 728a15, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 213, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.3.}}
**B.II** **undo** in various senses:
**B.II.1** **unloose,** ζώνην{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 237}}; in _middle_, **unwind** a cocoon, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 551b14.}}
**B.II.2** Astrology texts, **nullify,** of planetary influence, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 133 (_passive_).}}
**B.II.3** **dissolve** matter **into its elements,** ἐς αὐτὰ ταῦτα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 102d}}:—_passive_, of snow, **melt,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.898a.}}
**B.II.3.b** **resolve into its elements,** οὐ καλὸν ἁρμονίην ἀναλυέμεν ἀνθρώποιο{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 102}}:— **investigate analytically,** διάγραμμα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1112b20, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.792d, etc.}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 1.4}}; ὁ -όμενος τόπος the treasury **of analysis,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 634.2; ἀναλύοντες καὶ ἀναλυόμενοι 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 2; ἀ. τοὺς μύθους ἐς λόγους πιθανούς 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.74d.}}
**B.II.4** in the Logic of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}}, **reduce** a syllogism, {{Refs|“APr.” 47a4, +others; compare ἀνάλυσις 1.4.}}
**B.II.5** **reduce,** σχοινία εἰς ὀργυιάς{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “*Geometry texts” 5.8.}}
**B.II.6** Grammars, **resolve,** κτητικὰ εἰς γενικάς{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 292.17.}}
**B.II.7** **do away, cancel,** μόρσιμ᾽ ἀ. Ζεὺς οὐ τολμᾷ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Pae.” 6.94, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.218, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 25, etc.}}: mostly in _middle_, **cancel faults,** πάντα ταῦτα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.18; ἁμαρτίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.34}}; ἀλλύοιτό κα τὸ χρέος **discharge** the debt, probably in {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“GDI” 1151 (6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus).}}
**B.II.8** **suspend,** τὰ περὶ κυνηγέσιον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.34.}}
**B.II.9** **solve the problem** of a thing, τὸν Ἰνδὸν ἀ. **trace** its **source,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.133c.}}
**B.II.10** **release from a spell,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Vit.Auct.” 25, compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Her.fragment” 6.}}
**B.II.11** **relieve,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 133 (_passive_).}}
**B.III** _intransitive_, **loose from moorings, weigh anchor,** and so, **depart, go away,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.69.14, 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 42.8, etc.: _metaphorically_, of death, ἐς θεοὺς ἀνέλυσα “epigram Gr.” 340.7 (from Macestus)}}: _absolutely_, **die,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Phil.1.23, “IG” 14.1794; ἀ. ἐκ τοῦ ζῆν 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 2.}}
**B.III.2** **return,** {{LXX+NT|NT.Luke.12.36; ἐξ ᾅδου LXX.Wis.2.1.}}

361:
** ἀναμάρτ-ητος**, ον,
**making no mistake, unerring,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.22, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 339b.}}
**2** in moral sense, **blameless,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 16 (_comparative_),compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.10, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 487, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Sto.Herc.” 339.17}}; **sinless,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.John.8.7, cf. Aristeas 252, 1st c.AD: Musonius Philosophus “fragment” 2p.6 H}}.; ἀ. πολιτεία a **faultless** form of government, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1275b2}}; ἀ. πρός τινα **having done no wrong to** a person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.117; τινί 5.39}}; ἀ. τινός **guiltless of** a thing, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.155: τὸ -ητότατον, ={ἀναμαρτησία}, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 6.7, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 477e; πρὸς τὸ ἀ.}}]**to preserve from error,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1155a13}}. _adverb_ **-τως** **without fail, unerringly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.8.5}}; **without making a mistake,** Ps.-{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor Aphr. “in SE” 15.33}}; **inoffensively,** [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator] 61.21.}}
**II** of things, **not done by fault, done unavoidably,** συμφορά{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.11.}}
**II.2** **unfailing,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-4th c.AD(?): Dionysius Byzantius Geographus 17.}}

362:
** ἀναμένω**,
_poetry_ ἀμμένω, _Aeolic dialect_ ὀμμένω {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 41}}:— **wait for, await,** ἀνέμεινα.. Ἠῶ δῖαν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.342}}; νύκτα, τὸν ἥλιον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.42, 54; τέλος δίκης 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 243; frequently in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus, and _Attic dialect_ Prose:}} —οὐδὲ.. ἐπιθυμίαν ἀναμένεις **dost** not **wait for** desire to arise, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.30, compare “Smp.” 4.41; ἀ. τινά 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.57}}; **face** an enemy **in battle,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.31}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, οὐκ ἀ. τοὺς Ἕλληνας μάχης ἄρξαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.15: ἀ. φῦναι τὰς τρίχας [prev. author] 5.35; ἀ. ἡμέραν γενέσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.135, compare 120: followed by _relative_ clauses, ἀ. ἕως ἄν.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 209a; ἀ. αὐτοὺς ἔστ᾽ ἐμφάγοιέν τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.44}}; ποῖ χρῆν ἀναμεῖνα; i. e. ἐς τίνα χρόνο;{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 526}}: _absolutely_, **wait, stay,** Ἑρμῆς.. οὐκέτ᾽ ἀμμένει{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1397, compare 1389, “Tr.” 528, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 175}}: with _participle_, πεινῶν ἀ.{{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 777.}}
**2** **put off, delay,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.10}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.224.}}

363:
** ἀναμιμνήσκω**,
_future_ ἀναμνήσω, _poetry_ ἀμμνήσω: _Aeolic dialect_ _aorist_ _active_ ὀμναῖσαι{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.10}}: _aorist_ _infinitive_ _passive_ ὀμνάσθην{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.26}}:— **remind** one **of** a thing, with _double_ _accusative_, ταῦτά μ᾽ ἀνέμνησας{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.211. compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.140, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1133, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.6}}: but also with _genitive_ _of things_, μή μ᾽ ἀναμνήσῃς κακῶν{{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1045, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 246a: with _accusative_ _person_ only, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica [prev. cited]}}
**2** with _accusative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **remind** one to do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.54.}}
**3** with _accusative_ _of things_ only, **recall to memory, make mention of,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.11, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.213.}}
**4** followed by _conjunction_, ἀ. ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89, etc.; ὡς εἶχε τὰ πράγματ᾽ ἀναμνῆσαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.17.}}
**5** ἀναμιμνῄσκων, ὁ, **remembrancer,** {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.20.24, +others}}
**II** _passive_, **remember, recall to mind,** τινός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.151, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.54, etc.}}; less frequently τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 661, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 72e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.26; περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 329a}}: followed by a _relative_, ἀναμνησθέντας οἷα ἐπάσχετε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.109: _absolutely_, [prev. author] 3.51, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 552.}}
**II.2** ἀ. νοσήματος have a **relapse,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(2).423.}}

364:
** ἀνάμνη-σις**, εως, ἡ,
(ἀναμιμνῄσκω) **calling to mind, reminiscence,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 72e, 92d, “Phlb.” 34c (_plural_), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Memoria” 451a21}}; ἀ. τινος λαβεῖν **recall** it **to memory,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 2.628.20}}; ἀναμνήσεις θυσιῶν **reminders to the gods** of sacrifices offered, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.39.}}
**2** **memorial sacrifice,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Num.10.10, cf. NT.Luke.22.19.}}
**3** παλίνδρομος ἀ., of the moon, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 6.}}

365:
** ἀνανε-όομαι**,
_future_ **-ώσομαι**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 22.7.1, +others}}: _aorist_ ἀνενεωσάμην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.43, 46, poetry}}_infinitive_ ἀννεώσασθαι, see below:—**renew,** τὸν ὅρκον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.18; τὴν προξενίαν [prev. work] 43}}; τὰς σπονδάς{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 80; φιλίαν [prev. author] 7.33, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.121}}; ὁμόνοιάν τινι Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.167; συμμαχίαν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus [same place], +others}}
**II** κἀννεώσασθαι λόγους **revive** them, probably to be read instead of καὶ νεώσασθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 396, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 722, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.36.7.}}—_active_ frequently in {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX, LXX.Job.33.24, +others, “IG” 14.1078a, “Delph.” 3(1) No.60, compare “SIG” 478.503 (3rd c.BC), 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 391}}:—_passive_, {{NT|NT.Eph.4.23.}}

366:
** ἀνανήφω**,
**become sober again, come to one's senses,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 847b9; ἐκ μέθης 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.35, cf. Lynceus Comicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.109e; ἐκ τοῦ οἴνου 1st c.BC: Nicolaus Damascenus Historicus p.7 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; **return to sobriety of mind,** {{NT|NT.2Tim.2.26}}; **recover from a swoon,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 3.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 4.77.}}
**2** _transitive_, **make sober again,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Bis Acc.” 17.}}

370:
** ἀνάξιος**, ον,
(ἄναξ)
**B** **kingly, royal,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.630.}}

371:
Included with: ** ἀνάξιος**, ον,
(ἄναξ)
**B** **kingly, royal,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.630.}}

372:
** ἀνά-παυσις**,
_poetry_ ἄμπ-, εως, ἡ, **repose, rest,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 12.2, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.52, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 11, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 12.6}}: especially **relaxation, recreation,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 59c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.47.}}
**2** with _genitive_ _of things_, **rest from** a thing, κακῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.20; πολέμου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 2.11; κακῶν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.61U.; λειτουργίας “PFlor.” 57.56.}}
**3** Rhet., **cadence** of a period, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.1, +others}}

373:
** ἀνα-παύω**,
_poetry_ and _Ionic dialect_ ἀμπ-, _future_ _middle_ ἀναπαύσομαι: _aorist_ ἀνεπαυσάμην _Attic dialect_ and Hellenistic (but ἀνεπαύθημεν{{LXX|LXX.Lam.5.5)}}: later, _aorist_ _passive_ ἀναπάη{{Refs|“IG” 14.158:}}_future_ _passive_ ἀναπαήσομαι variant {{NT|NT.Rev.14.13, +others}}: _present_ _middle_ ἀναπάεται{{Refs|“IG” 14.1717, compare “PTeb.” 264}}:—**make to cease, stop** or **hinder from** a thing, χειμῶνος.. ὅς ῥά τε ἔργων ἀνθρώπους ἀνέπαυσεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.550}}; ἀ. τινὰ τοῦ πλάνου **give** him **rest** from wandering, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1113}}; τοὺς λειτουργοῦντας ἀ. (i.e. τῶν ἀναλωμάτων) **to relieve** them from.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.25 compare 42.}}
**2** with _accusative_ only, **put an end to,** βοήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1262}}; more frequently **rest, make to halt,** ἀ. στράτευμα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.4; κατὰ μέρος τοὺς ναύτας ἀ. [prev. author] “HG” 6.2.29; κάματον ἵππων ἀ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 192 (Lyric poetry); σῶμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1353}}; εἴδωλον ἀ. ἐπὶ ἅμαξαν **lay** it in **a reposing posture,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 12.64, compare “NA” 7.29: _absolutely_, ἀνάπαυσον}}**give me rest,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tyr.” 21.}}
**3** **bring to a close,** τὸν λόγον{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.8.}}
**4** rarely _intransitive_ in sense of _middle_, **take rest,** ἀναπαύοντες ἐν τῷ μέρει{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.11; ἡσυχίαν εἶχε καὶ ἀνέπαυεν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.21.}}
**5** of land, **cause to lie fallow**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 400.10 (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 105.3 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II** in _middle_ and _passive_, **take rest,** ἀναπαύου κακῶν **take rest from**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 297}}; ἀπὸ ναυμαχίας ἀ. **rest** after a sea-fight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.73; ἐκ μακρᾶς ὁδοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 106a}}; ἀπ᾽ ἄγρας κεκμακὼς ἀμπαύσεται [Πάν] {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.17}}; especially of troops, **halt, rest,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.3, etc.}}; ἀναπεπ. τῶν εἰσφορῶν **to be relieved from**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.20.}}
**II.2** _absolutely_, **take one's rest, sleep,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.12, 2.95, +others, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 211, variant in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 695, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.12, etc.}}
**II.2.b** of land, **lie fallow,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.11.}}
**II.2.c** of the dead, ἀμπ. σὺν φιλίῃ ξυνῶς ἀλόχῳ{{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 520.5 (from Thessalonica)}}; ὧδε ἀναπάεται{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 14.1717, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 15.1; ἀ. τοῦ βίου 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus “Allegoriae Quaestiones Homericae” 68, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.15.2}}; ἀ. alone, **die,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] 1.4.7, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.110f; ἀ. τὸν βίον “POxy.” 1121.12(3rd c.AD).}}
**II.2.d** **regain strength,** uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6 1.11.}}
**II.3** **rest** or **settle** upon an object, τὸ τοῦ Θεοῦ πνεῦμα ἐφ᾽ ὑμᾶς ἀναπαύεται {{LXX+NT|NT.1Pet.4.14, cf. LXX.Isa.11.2}}; of shadows, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Comm. in Mathematics texts” 8.}}

374:
** ἀναπείθω**
{{Refs 4th c.AD+|(4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus ἀμπ- “SIG” 306.59)}}, **persuade, convince,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.52, +others}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.84.}}
**2** **persuade, move** to do a thing, with _accusative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.124, 156, etc.}}; followed by _conjunction_, ἀ. ὡς χρή.. {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.123; ἀ. λόγῳ ὅκως.. 1.37: with double _accusative_, ἀ. τινά τι}} **persuade** one **of** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 77, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 9.438 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus).}}
**3** **seduce, mislead,** τινά{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.148, 5.66, etc.}}; ἀ. χρήμασι, δώροις, **bribe,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 622, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.3}}; χρυσίον διδοὺς ἀναπείσεις ὅπως.. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 473, compare “PMagd.” 14.3, NT.Act.18.13}}:—_passive_, ἀναπεπεισμένος **bribed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 101.}}

375:
** ἀναπέμπω**,
_poetry_ ἀμπ-, **send up,** κάτωθεν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 382 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 585}}; Ἁφαίστοιο κρουνοὺς ἀ. **sends forth**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.26}}; χθὼν ἠρινὰ φύλλ᾽ ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 9.46; παντοῖα φύματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 85c}}:—_middle_, **send up from oneself,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.5.}}
**2** **send up** to higher ground, εἰς τὰς ἄκρας{{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 7.5.34}}; especially from the coast inland, into Central Asia, ἀ. ὡς βασιλέα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.67, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.98}}; to the metropolis, εἰς τὴν Ῥώμην{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.7.12, etc.}}
**3** **remit, refer** to higher authority, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.57 (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 7.7 (2nd c.BC)}}; ψήφισμα πρὸς βασιλέα{{NT|“OGI” 329.51; τινὰ πρός τινα NT.Luke.23.7; τινά τινι NT.Philm.12}}; of a higher authority **referring** to delegates, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 613.4 (2nd c.AD), compare 19i20, “PLond.” 2.196, 11 (2nd c.AD)}}; **refer** to a book, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).663, etc.}}
**4** **trace up** one's pedigree, γένος εἴς τινα{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.83.}}
**5** **transmit,** in _passive_, τῶν κατ᾽ ὄψιν ἀναπεμπομένων{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 11.7; αἰσθήσεων ἀναπεμπομένων 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.4.42.}}
**II** **send back,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 7(6).10}}: _metaphorically_, **send back** in discussion to something previously said, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Topicorum libros octo commentaria - pars” 445.15.}}
**II.2** **refer,** τὰ εἰς τὸ θεἰον -όμενα{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 194, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.43}}; **ascribe,** τι ἐπί τι{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 37; τίτινι “Corpus Hermeticum” 18.12.}}
**II.3** **throw back** the accent, of enclitics, Hdn.Gr.2.828.

377:
** ἀναπίπτω**,
_poetry_ ἀμπ-, **fall back,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1599, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 410}}; **lay oneself back,** like rowers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 345, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 8.8; ἀ. ὑπτία 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254b}}, compare e; of riders, ὑπτίους ἀναπεπτωκότας ἐλαύνειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 3.14.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **fall back, give ground,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70}}; **flag, lose heart,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.224}}; ταῖς σπουδαῖς (_vulgar form_ σπονδ-) ἀναπεπτωκέναι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.53.}}
**2.b** _perfect_ _participle_ ἀναπεπτωκώς **lifeless,** of style, σχῆμα ἀ.{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “τέχναι ῥητορικαί” 2p.518S., +others}}
**3** of a plan, **to be given up,** ἀνεπεπτώκει τὰ τῆς ἐξόδου{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.163.}}
**4** ἀ. ἀπ᾽ οἴκων **to be banished** from one's house, poetical cited in Athenagoras**pro Christo** 22.
**5** **recline** at meals, like{ἀνάκειμαι}, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 293, “Comica Adespota” 638, “PPar.” 51.4, NT.Mark.6.40, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 23.}}
**5.b** **take to one's bed** in sickness, {{Refs|“PMag.Leid.V.” 11.1.}}
**6** **recoil,** of the arms of torsion-engines, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 100.2.}}

378:
** ἀναπληρ-όω**,
**fill up** a void, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 81b, compare 78d}}; τὸ κεχηνὸς τῆς ἑρμηνείας, τοῦ ῥυθμοῦ, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 266.22, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 1}}:—_passive_, **to be filled up,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 306b4.}}
**2** **make up, supply,** εἴ τι ἐξέλιπον ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 188e; τὴν ἔνδειαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1318b22}}; τοὺς.. ἀμόρφους ἀνατληροῖ ἡ τοῦ λέγειν πιθανότης **compensates** them, {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 101:}} —_middle_, δώματ᾽ ἀ. **fill their** houses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 907.}}
**3** **fill up** the numbers of a body, τὴν βουλήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 11, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.24}}; ἀ. τὴν συνηγορίαν **fill** the place of advocate (left vacant by another), {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Crassus” 3, cf. NT.1Cor.14.16.}}
**4** **pay in full,** τὰς ὠνάς, of tax-farmers, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PPar.” 62.5.3 (2nd c.BC)}}:—in _middle_, **get paid, receive,** ἕως ἀνεπληρώσατο τὴν προῖκα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.13.}}
**5** **use expletive particles,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 58.}}
**6** **fulfil,** ἀναπληροῦται ἡ προφητεία{{NT|NT.Matt.13.14}}; of a task, **perform,** {{Refs|PPetr. 3p.104.}}
**II** _passive_, **to be restored to its former size** or **state,** ἀνεπληρώθη ὁ ἥλιος, after an eclipse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.28; ἀναπληρουμένης τῆς φύσεως}}**being in process of restoration,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1153a2, compare “HA” 548b18.}}

380:
** ἀνα-πτύσσω**,
_perfect_ _infinitive_ _passive_ ἀνεπτύχθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 357}}: _aorist_ _passive_ ἀνεπτύχθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ κρισίων”}} 3, but **-επτύγην** {{Refs|“Int.” 48}}:—**unfold** the rollson which books were written, **open for reading,** ἀ. τὸ βιβλίον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.125, compare 48; δέλτων ἀναπτύσσοιμι γῆρυν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 370: also ἀ. πύλας, κύτος}} **undo, open,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1286, “Ion” 39; χλαμύδα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 42; even χεῖλος 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 3.247}}; ἀναπτύξασ χέρας with arms **outspread,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1190; σεισμοὶ -ξαντες τὴν ἁρμονίαν τῶν ὀρῶν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.17}}:—_middle_, **fold up,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 664b27, +others}}
**b** **cut open,** of freshly killed animals,{{Refs 1st c.AD+|Pherecyd 97 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 17.3, “PMag.Leid.V.” 10.1, etc.}}
**c** **ruminate, chew,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.137.}}
**2** **unfold, disclose,** πᾶν ἀ. πάθος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 254, 294; πάντ᾽ ἀναπτύσσει χρόνος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 301; ἀ. πρὸς φῶς [prev. author] “El.” 639, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1256; φρένα πρός τινα [prev. author] “Tr.” 662: in later Prose, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Antro Nympharum - Porphyrii Opuscula” 4.}}
**II** as military term, τὴν φάλαγγα ἀ. **fold back** the phalanx, i.e. **deepen** it by countermarching from front to rear, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.3}}; conversely, τὸ κέρας ἀ. **open out** the wing, i.e. **extend** the line by countermarching from rear to front, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.10.9, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 23, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 9.5.}}

381:
** ἀνάπτω**,
**make fast on** or **to,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (only 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”), ἐκ δ᾽ αὐτοῦ [ἱστοῦ] πείρατ᾽ ἀνῆπτον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.179, compare 51, 162; πρυμνήσι᾽ ἀνάψαι 9.137: with _dative_, γαίῃ 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.177; ἀ. τι πρός τι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1012:}} — _middle_, ἐκ τοῦδ᾽ ἀναψόμεσθα πρυμνήτην κάλων to him will we **moor** our bark, i.e. he shall be our protector, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Med.” 770, etc.}}; θεοῖσι κῆδος ἀνάψασθαι **form a close connexion** with.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 845}}; χάριτας ἔς τινα ἀ. **confer** favours on.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 569}}; also, **fasten to oneself,** ἐπιστολὴν ἐκ τῶν δακτύλων ἀ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.36}}; **take in tow,** ναῦν{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.19, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 8; τὸ κράτος 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.474}}:—_passive_, **to be fastened** or **fasten oneself on to, cling to,** with _genitive_, πέπλων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 629: with _dative_, κίοσιν [prev. work] 1038 (probably, Lyric poetry)}}; **have** a thing **fastened** on one, περιβόλαι᾽ ἀνήμμεθα {{Refs|[prev. work] 549.}}
**2** **hang up** in a temple, **offer up,** like{ἀνατίθημι}, πολλὰ δ᾽ ἀγάλματ᾽ ἀνῆψεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.274, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 572, 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 853, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.11, 3rd-4th c.AD: Tryphiodorus Epicus 256.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **fasten upon, attach to,** μῶμον ἀνάψαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.86}}; αἷμα ἀ. τινί a charge of bloodshed, uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1196, compare 1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 70, etc.; κήδε᾽ ἀνῆπταί τινι 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.245}}; **ascribe, refer to,** τοὺς λόγους εἰς ἀριθμοὺς ἀ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1078b22; ἀρχήν, αἰτίαν ἀ. εἰς τὸν Πύθιον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 6, etc.}}; χάριν ἀ. τινί **ascribe** a favour to him, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 46; τὴν χάριν εἰς Καίσαρα πάντων ἀνῆπτεν [prev. author] “Brut.” 6; τοῖς ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ τὴν εὐδαιμονίαν ἀνάψασι 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 2.3}}:—_middle_, **attach oneself,** πρὸς πολλούς{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1457.8.}}
**II** **light up, kindle,** λύχνα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.133; πῦρ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1137; φῶς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 39b; πυρὶ ἀ. δόμους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1594: _metaphorically_, νέφος οἰμωγῆς ὡς τάχ᾽ ἀνάψει [prev. author] “Med.” 107}}:—_passive_, **to be kindled,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.31, etc.}}
**II.2** **inflame with anger,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 68.35}}:—_passive_,{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. work] 33.15, 3rd c.AD: Pseudo-Callisthenes Historicus 3.22}}; **excite** emotionally, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 1425.20.}}
**II.3** _intransitive_, **to be lighted** up, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 841a32.}}

382:
** ἀνᾰρίθμ-ητος**, ον,
**not to be counted, countless,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.25, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 7.190, 211, +others}}; of time, **immeasurable,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 646.}}
**2** **unregarded,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 837, “Hel.” 1679.}}

383:
** ἀνα-σείω**,
_poetry_ ἀνασσείω, _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ἀνασσείασκε{{Refs|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 403}}:— **shake back,** ἀνασείοντά τε κόμας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 240}}; **swing to and fro, brandish,** αἰγίδα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 344}}; ἀ. τὰς χεῖρας **wave** the hands, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.38; ἀ. φοινικίδας 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.51.}}
**2** **brandish at** one, **threaten with,** εἰσαγγελίαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.47; βοὴν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 347}} (παρὰ προσδοκίαν).
**3** **shake out,** ὑδρίαν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 2.104a36 (4th c.BC)}}; πάντα κάλων **shake out** every reef, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.327, +others; ἀ. τὰ ἱστία 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 6.12, compare “VS” 2.32; πάσας τὰς ἡνίας 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.214; τὴν χλαμύδα 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.6.}}
**II** **stir up,** τὰ πλήθη{{NT+5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.290 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.81, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.91, NT.Mark.15.11, NT.Luke.23.5}}:—_passive_, **to be incited, encouraged,** with _infinitive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 28.20 (2nd c.BC).}}

384:
** ἀνασκευ-άζω**,
**pack up the baggage** (τὰ σκεύη), and so, **carry away, remove,** τὴν ἀγορὰν εἴσω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.2.8, etc.}}; ἀ. τοὺς Ἀθηναίους ἐκ θαλάττης **divert** them from naval enterprise, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.17.3, compare 1.25.7}}:— in _middle_, **break up camp, march away,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.18; κατεσκευάζετο καὶ πάλιν ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.5.2, etc.}}
**2** **dismantle** a place, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.116}}:—in _middle_, **dismantle one's** house or city, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.18.}}
**3** **waste, ravage,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.25 (_passive_)}}; ἀ. τὰς συνθήκας **break** them, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.31.6}}:—in _middle_, τάφον{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.578f.}}
**4** _passive_, **to be bankrupt,** τῆς τραπέζης ἀνασκευασθείσης{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.9, compare 49.68}}; οἱ ἀνεσκευασμένοι τῶν τραπεζιτῶν **broken** bankers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. passage]: _metaphorically_, ἀνεσκευάσμεθα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 602.}}
**5** of logicians, **demolish** opponent's arguments, definitions, etc., opposed to κατασκευάζειν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 43a2, compare “Rh.” 1401b4, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.2.18, 3rd c.BC: Polystratus Epicureus p.24 W.}}
**6** **reverse** a decision or judgement, Vett. Val.{{Refs|283.23 (_passive_): _metaphorically_, ἀ. ψυχάς}}**disturb,** opposed to οἰκοδομέω, {{NT|NT.Act.15.24, compare 9.31.}}
**7** in Medicine texts, **remove,** νόσον{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.8.}}
**II** **build again, remodel,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.1.5}}:—also in _middle_, **build,** οἴκους{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 6.5.2.}}

385:
** ἀνα-σπάω**,
_poetry_ ἀνσπ-, **draw, pull up,** σπυρίδα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.16, compare 4.154; βύβλον ἐκ τῶν ἑλέων [prev. author] 2.92}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“BGU” 1041.8 (3rd c.AD).}}
**b** **draw** a ship **up** on land, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.27, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.188, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.9.}}
**2** **draw, suck up greedily,** ὅταν αἷμ᾽ ἀνασπάσῃ κόνις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 647; ἀ. ὑγρόν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 22}}; ἀ. ποτόν, τροφήν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 495a26, “PA” 661a19}}; ὕδωρ ἀ. **draw** water, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.97.}}
**3** **draw back,** τὴν χεῖρα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 691}}:—so in _middle_, ἐκ χροὸς ἔγχος ἀνεσπάσατ᾽{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.574.}}
**4** **tear up, pull down,** τὰ ἀγάλματα ἐκ τῶν βάθρων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.86; τὴν σκηνήν [prev. author] 7.119; τὸ σταύρωμα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.100; τύμβους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1381, compare “Ba.” 949; δένδρα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 497b29, +others; τὰς σανιδας τῆς γεφύρας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.5.5; πυλίδας [prev. author] 5.39.4, etc.}}
**5** _metaphorically_, ἀνασπᾶν λόγους, in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 302}}, **draw forth** words, **utter wild, incoherent** words; ἀνασπῶντ᾽ αὐτοπρέμνοις τοῖς λόγοισιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 903}}:—the phrase may be explanation from {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 180a}} (ὥσπερ ἐκ φαρέτρας ῥηματίσκια.. ἀνασπῶντες) and {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 429}} (πόθεν.. τούτους ἀνεσπάκασιν οὗτοι τοὺς λόγου;); so ἀ. γνωμίδιον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 49D.}}
**6** τὰς ὀφρῦς ἀνασπᾶν **pucker** the eyebrows, and so put on a grave important air, τὰς ὀφρῦς ἀνεσπακὼς ὥσπερ τι δεινὸν ἀγγελῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 1069, compare 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 16, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.314; ἔβλεψε νᾶπυ καὶ τὰ μέτωπ᾽ ἀνέσπασεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 631; μέχρι νεφέων τὴν ὀφρὺν ἀ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 174, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 3.10; οἱ τὰς ὀφρῦς ἀνεσπασμένοι πρὸς τὸν κρόταφον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 812b27.}}
**II** **retract,** ὁ στόμαχος αὐτὸς ἑαυτὸν ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἐπικυήσιος” 22, “Steril.” 217.}}
**III** **carry away from home,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 28 codices}}

386:
** ἀνά-στᾰσις**, εως,
_Ionic dialect_ ιος, ἡ,
**I** _active_, (ἀνίστημι) **making to stand** or **rise up, raising up** the dead, ἀνδρὸς δ᾽ ἐπειδὰν αἷμ᾽ ἀνασπάσῃ κόνις.. οὔτις ἔστ᾽ ἀ.{{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 648; ἔλαβον.. ἐξ ἀναστάσεως τοὺς νεκροὺς αὐτῶν NT.Heb.11.35.}}
**I.2** **making to rise and leave their place, removal,** as of suppliants, ἀ. ἐκ τοῦ ἱεροῦ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.133}}; ἀ. τῆς Ἰωνίας **removal** of the Greeks from Ionia [for safety], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.106}}: mostly in bad sense, **desolation,** ἅλωσιν Ἰλίου τ᾽ ἀνάστασιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Ages.” 589; πόλεων ἀ. [prev. author] “Pers.” 107, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 364; τῆς πατρίδος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.5}}; **disturbance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Decent..” 3 (_plural_).}}
**I.3** **setting up, erection,** τειχῶν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.72; τροπαίου 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.873a; εἰκόνος “GDI” 3505.20 (from Cnidus), compare “IPE” 12.34.8 (from Olbia), 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 4.11.2; οἰκοδομημάτων 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Phal.” 1.3 (_plural_).}}
**II** (ἀνίσταμαι) **standing** or **rising up,** πόδες ἀναστάσεως χάριν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Spiritu” 485a18, compare [prev. author] “fragment” 156.}}
**II.2** **rising and moving off, removal,** στρατεύματος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.75, compare 2.14.}}
**II.3** **rising up,** ἐξ ὕπνου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 276.}}
**II.3.b** especially for the stool, uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.7.1}}: hence, **motions,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|[prev. author] “Coac.” 605, 4th c.AD: Dieuches Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 4.6.2.}}
**II.3.c** **rising again** after a fall, {{NT|NT.Luke.2.34.}}
**II.3.d** **rising from the dead,** Τυνδάρεω{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 45}}; εἰς ἀνάστασιν [perhaps βλέποντες] {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“IGRom.” 4.743 (Eumeneia, 3rd c.AD)}}: frequently in N.T., {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Matt.22.23, +others; ἀ. νεκρῶν NT.Act.23.6; ἀ. ζωῆς, κρίσεως NT.John.5.29; ἀπὸ σώματος ἀ. 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.6.6.}}

388:
** ἀνασταυρ-όω**,
= {ἀνασταυρίζω}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.125, 6.30, +others; identical with ἀνασκολοπίζω, 9.78}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.110, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 473c}}.
**II** in Rom. times, **affix to a cross, crucify,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.11.5, +others, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 6, +others}}
**II.2** **crucify afresh,** {{NT|NT.Heb.6.6.}}

389:
** ἀνα-στενάζω**,
={ἀναστένω}, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86, 6.80, NT.Mark.8.12: with _accusative_ _cognate_, τοῖά μοι ἀ. ἐχθοδοπά}} such bitter words **didst** thou **groan forth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 930.}}
**II** with _accusative_ _person_, **groan for, lament,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 335, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 118 (Lyric poetry).}}

390:
** ἀναστρέφω**,
_poetry_ ἀνστρέφω, _perfect_ ἀνέστροφα variant in {{Refs|Theognetus Comicus 1.8}}, _3rd.pers.plural_ ἀνέστροφαν{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus “fragment” 17.30}}:—**turn upside down,** μήπως.. δίφρους ἀνστρέψειαν **might upset** them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.436; ὁ θεὸς πάντ᾽ ἀ. πάλιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 331; ἀ. γένος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1240}}; τὴν ζοήν Cerc.[same place]; ἀ. καρδίαν **upset** the stomach, i.e. cause sickness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49}}; **reverse,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 333, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 779}}:—_passive_, _future_ ἀναστραφήσεσθαι τὰ τῆς Ἑλλάδος πράγματα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.64}}: _perfect_ ἀνεστράφθαι τῆς πολιτείας{{Refs|[prev. author] 6.66 codices}}; ὄρος ἀνεστραμμένον ἐν τῇ ζητήσει **turned up** by digging, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.47, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 16.12.}}
**2** **invert** order of words or statements, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 11, +others, 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.11:}} —also in _passive_, with _reference_ to ἐπαναστροφή{{Refs|(which see), [prev. work] 12.}}
**3** ={ἀρνεῖσθαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1012.}}
**II** **turn back,** {{Refs|Comica Adespota 22.73D.}}; **bring back,** τινὰ ἐξ Ἅιδου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 449, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1228; ἀ. δίκην τινί [prev. author] “Ba.” 793}}; ὄμμ᾽ ἀ. κύκλῳ **to roll** it **about,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hel.” 1557.}}
**II.2** _intransitive_, **turn back, retire,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80, etc.}}; especially in _participle_, ἀναστρέψας ἀπήλαυνεν {{Refs|X “An.” 1.4.5, etc.}}; but also, **rally,** of troops, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.43, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.21, compare 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 111.1:}} —ἀναστρέφον, τό, see. a)nakukliko/s.
**III** in Grammars, **write with anastrophe,** as πέρι for περί, {{Refs|Hdn.Gr. 2.52}},66:—_passive_, {{Refs|1.481, +others}}
**III.2** in Mathematics texts, ἀναστρέψαντι **convertendo,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 5.19 “Cor.”}}; so in Logic, οἱ ἀντιστρέφοντες οὐχ οἱ ἀναστρέφοντες ἀλλήλοις λόγοι συναληθεύονται{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.465.}}
**B** _passive_, see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.}}
**B.II** **dwell in** a place, ἀλλά τιν᾽ ἄλλην γαῖαν ἀναστρέφομαι **go to** a place **and dwell there,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.326, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Lavacrum Palladis” 76, “Aet.” 1.1.6 (so ἀναστρέφειν πόδα ἐν γῇ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1176); ἀναττρέφεσθαι ἐν Ἀργει [prev. author] “Tr.” 993}}; ἐν φανερῷ, ἐν μέσῳ, **go about** in public, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 558a; ἀ. ταύτῃ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.94; ἐν εὐφροσύναις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Ag.” 9.4; ἐν τοῖς ἤθεσι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 865e}}; ἀ. ἐν ξυμμαχίᾳ **continue in** an alliance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.3.2; ἀ. ἐν γεωργίᾳ}}**to be engaged in..,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Oec.” 5.13; ἐπὶ κυνηγεσίαις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 32.15.9}}; ἀ. ἔν τινι **dwell upon,** in writing, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Cit. 2}}: generally, **conduct oneself, behave,** ὡς δεστότης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.14; οὑτωσί 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1103b20}}; θρασέως, ἀχαρίστως καὶ ἀσεβῶς εἴς τινα, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.9.7, 23.17.10; ἐν ταῖς ἀρχαῖς ὁσίως “IG” 12(7).233 (from Amorgos)}}; ὡς τὰ παιδία{{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 29.3; πῶς δεῖ ἐν οἴκῳ θεοῦ ἀ. NT.1Tim.3.15.}}
**B.II.2** **revolve,** like the sun in the heavens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.8.}}
**B.III** of soldiers, **face about, rally,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 1.10.12, “HG” 6.2.20, etc.}}
**B.III.2** **to be reversed** or **inverted,** ἐμοὶ τοῦτ᾽ ἀνέστραπται{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hier.” 4.5, compare “Cyr.” 8.8.13, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mechanica” 854a10.}}
**B.III.3** **return,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 271a}}; **retreat,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 621b34.}}

391:
** ἀνα-στροφή**, ἡ,
**turning upside down, upsetting, overthrow,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 301 (_plural_)}}; μοῖραν εἰς ἀ. δίδωσι, ={ἀναστρέφει}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Andr.” 1007}}; **disorder, confusion,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 26.22.}}
**2** **turning back, return,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 226}}; πολλὰς ἀ. ποιούμενος, of a hunter, making many **casts backward,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.25}}; **wheeling round,** of a horse, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.Mag.” 3.14}}; of soldiers in battle, whether to flee or rally, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 5.4.8; μηκέτι δοῦναι αὐτοῖς ἀ.}}**time to rally,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 4.3.6, compare “Ages.” 2.3}}; especially of the **reversal** of a wheeling movement, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 10.6, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 25.7, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 21.4}}; of a ship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89}}; ἐξ ἀ. **turning back,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.54.4}}; κατ᾽ ἀναστροφήν **conversely,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.430.}}
**3** in Grammars, **throwing back of the accent,** as in Prepositions after their case, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 308.15, etc.}}
**4** Rhet., ={ἐπαναστροφή}, **repetition** of words which close one sentence at the beginning of another, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.12, etc.}}
**4.b** **inversion** of the natural order, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 71.18, 5th-6th c.AD: Phoebammon Rhetor “de Figuris - Rhet.” 1.4, etc.; τῆς τάξεως 1st-2nd c.AD(?): Theon Rhetor “Progymnasmata - Rhet.” 4.}}
**5** in Mathematics texts, **conversion** of a ratio, ἀ. λόγου{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 5 “Def.” 16; κατ᾽ ἀναστροφήν 3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 1002.25.}}
**II** **dwelling in** a place, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.216a.}}
**II.2** **abode, haunt,** δαιμόνων ἀναστροφή{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 23.}}
**II.3** **mode of life, behaviour,** {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.82.1, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 0.64; -φὴν ποιεῖσθαι “IG” 2.477b12, compare “SIG” 491.5, LXX.Tob.4.14, NT.Gal.1.13, NT.Eph.4.22, +others; ἀ. πολιτική “PGiss.” 40ii29 (3rd c.AD)}}; ἐξημερωμένης -φῆς civilized **life,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Sto.Herc.” 339.19.}}
**II.4** **delay, respite, time for doing** a thing, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.66.3, +others, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 10.5.}}
**II.5** **occupation, concern,** περί τι τὰν ἀ. ἔχειν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 1, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.1425.6.}}
**II.6** **return, way back,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 631a26, compare “Pr.” 940b23.}}
**II.7** **recourse,** ἀ. λαμβάνειν πρός τι{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.112c.}}

393:
** ἀνατέλλω**,
_poetry_ ἀντ-, _aorist_ ἀνέτειλα: _perfect_ _participle_ ἀνατεταλκός{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.15.10}}:—**make to rise up,** τοῖσιν δ᾽ (i.e. ἵπποις) ἀμβροσίην ἀνέτειλενέμεσθυ{{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.777; Αἴγυπτος.. Δήμητρος ἀντέλλει στάχυν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 300, cf. LXX.Gen.3.18}}; ὕδωρ ἀ. **make** water **gush forth,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).75; τὸν ἥλιον NT.Matt.5.45:—so in}}_passive_, φλὸξ ἀνατελλομένα a flame **mounting up,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 4(3).83.}}
**2** **bring forth, give birth to** ἄντειλας Διόνυσον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 7(6).5; ἰούλους 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.44: of events, μυρί᾽ ἀπ᾽ αἰσχρῶν ἀνατέλλοντα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1139.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **rise, appear above the horizon,** of any heavenly body, as sun and moon, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.142, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1246, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 754; πρὸς ἠῶ τε καὶ ἥλιον ἀνατέλλοντα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.204, 4.40}}; also of constellations, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.959; ἠὼσἀντ. [prev. author] 2.1007}}; distinct from ἐπιτέλλω (which see), {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 8.4}}; compare ἀνατολή.
**II.b** ={ἐπιτέλλω}, of the Pleiades, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 13.25.}}
**II.2** of a river, **take its rise,** ἐκ ταύτης [γῆς λίμνης] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.52, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 14.16, etc.}}
**II.2.b** of persons, **originate,** ἐξ Ἰούδα ἀνατέταλκεν ὁ κύριος{{NT|NT.Heb.7.14.}}
**II.3** **grow,** of hair, ταρφὺς ἀντέλλουσα θρίξ{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 535}}; of teeth, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 501b29}}; **spring up,** of plants, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.1.6, +others; compare ἀνατολή 11.}}
**II.4** of mountains, **rise,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.501}}; but, **appear on the horizon,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 601.}}
**II.5** **rise up,** ἀνέτειλε σωτήρ probably in {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 978 (from Philae).}}

394:
** ἀνατίθημι**,
_perfect_ ἀνατέθηκα{{Refs|“SIG” 1018.9 (Pergam.)}}, etc.:—**lay upon,** once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἐλεγχείην ἀναθήσει μοι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.100}}; ἀ. ἄχθος **lay on** as a burden, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1056 (hexameters), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.30; κινδύνους ἰδιώταις ἀ. 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 9}}: in good sense, ἀ. κῦδός τινι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 5.8}}. b. _middle_, **put on board ship,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 5(1).1421 (from Cyparissia).}}
**2** in Prose, **refer, attribute,** a thing to a person, μεγάλα οἱ χρήματα ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.135}}; οὐ γὰρ ἄν οἱ πυραμίδα ἀνέθεσαν ποιήσασθαι would not **have attributed** to her the erection of the pyramid,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 134; φοίβῳ τήνδ᾽ ἀναθήσω πρᾶξιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1296}}; εἰμή, ὅταν.. εὖ πράξητε, ἐμοὶ ἀναθήσετε **will give** me **the credit of** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64; οὐ τῷ συμβούλῳ τὴν τοῦ κατορθοῦν ἀνέθηκε δύναμιν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.290; ἀ. τινὶ τὴν αἰτίαν τινός 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.37, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.10}}; also, **compare,** τινὰ εἴς τι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.261 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator b. ἀ. τινὶ ἅπαντα πράγματα}} **lay** them **upon** him, **entrust** them to him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1453, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.82.}}
**II** **set up as a votive gift, dedicate,** τινί τι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 658, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.30, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.159, 7.54, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1089, etc.; Ῥήνειαν ἀνέθηκε τῷ Ἀπόλλωνι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.13; ἀνάθημα ἀνατιθέναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.53, 2.182; ἀ. τι ἐς Δελφούς [prev. author] 1.92, 2.135, 182, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 235d, etc.; less frequently ἐν Δελφοῖς 5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 1 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 25}}; **dedicate** a book, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sull.” 6}}; ἀ. τινά **set up a statue of..**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 420 (Delos, 3rd c.BC)}}; incorrectly of burial, {{Refs|“OGI” 602 (from Jaffa)}}:— _passive_, ἀνατεθῆναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 849}}; compare ἀνάκειμαι.
**II.2** **set up, erect,** [στήλην] παρὰ βωμόν, νεών, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.93.10, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 14}}: _metaphorically_, **dedicate,** μακραγορίαν λύρᾳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.29}}; ἀ. τὰς ἀκοὰς τοῖς ἀκροάμασι **give** them **up** to, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.5.9.}}
**II.3** **set up and leave** in a place, ἀ. τινὰ ἐπὶ κρημνόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 69}}; ἀ. ζῶντα (on a cross) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.86.6.}}
**III** **put back,** τί γὰρ παρ᾽ ἦμαρ ἡμέρα τέρπειν ἔχει, προσθεῖσα κἀναθεῖσα τοῦ γε κατθανεῖ; pushing us forward or **moving** us **back** on the verge of death, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 476; compare 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.2.}}
**B** _middle_, **put upon for oneself,** ἀναθέσθαι τὰ σκεύη ἐπὶ τὰ ὑποζύγια{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.4}}; **pack** on one's cart, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.19}}; τοῖς ὤμοις ἀ. τινά **put on one's** shoulders, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.983b}}; frequently like _active_, ἀ. τινὰ ἐφ᾽ ἵππον{{Refs|[prev. author] “Art.” 11, etc.}}
**B.2** **impart, communicate** something **one's own,** τινί τι{{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Act.25.14, NT.Gal.2.2, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.772d.}}
**B.3** **remit, refer,** ἀ. περί τινος εἰς σύγκλητον **refer** the consideration of it to the Senate, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.46.11, compare 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Σαυνιτική” 4.}}
**B.II** **place differently, change about,** e.g. the men on a draught-board, ἀνὰ πάντα τιθεσθαι variant in Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.77.}}
**B.II.2** **take back a move** at πεττοί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hipparchus” 229e}}: hence _metaphorically_, **retract one's opinion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.44, compare 2.4.4; frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, ἀνατίθεσθαι ὅ τι δοκεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 462a, compare “Prt.” 354e. “Chrm.” 164d}}; οὐκ ἀνατίθεμυι μὴ οὐ.. **retract and say** this is not so, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Phd.” 87a; οὐκ ἀ. μὴ οὐ καλὼς λέγεσθαι [prev. author] “Men.” 89d; ἀνατιθέμενος τὸ διημαρτημένον 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pseudol.” 29.}}

395:
** ἀνατολή**,
_poetry_ ἀντ-, (ἀνατέλλω) **rising** above the horizon, of any heavenly body, e.g. the sun, frequently in _plural_, ἀντολαὶ ἠελίοιο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.4, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 504:—also in _sing._, ἀπ᾽ ἀνατολᾶς ἁλίου ἄχρι δύσεως “IG” 4.606; δύσεώς τε καὶ ἀνατολῆς ἡλίου καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἄστρων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 269a, compare “Lg.” 807e}}; distinct from ἐπιτολή (which see), {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 13.3.}}
**2** ={ἐπιτολή}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 457, “Ag.” 7; περὶ Ὠρίωνος ἀνατολήν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 361b23; ἀπὸ Πλειάδος ἀ. [prev. author] “HA” 599b11.}}
**3** **the quarter of sunrise, east,** opposed to δύσις, frequently in _plural_, ἀπὸ ἡλίου ἀνατολέων{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.8; ἡλίου πρὸς ἀντολάς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 707; without ἡλίου, πρὸς ἀνατολάς 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.15.2, “Mon.Anc.Gr.” 14.12; πρὸς τὰς ἀ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.14.4; ἀπὸ ἀνατολῶν LXX.Num.23.7, NT.Matt.2.1, etc.}}
**3.b** the **ascendant,** i.e. the point where the eastern horizon cuts the zodiac, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 20.}}
**3.c** phase of new moon when {{Refs 4th c.AD+|150 distant from sun, “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(4).204, 4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “G.” 3.}}
**4** in _plural_, **sources** of a river, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.17.4.}}
**II** **growing,** of the teeth, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 501b28}}; of the white at the root of the nails, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.146: _plural_, ἀγρὸς ἀνατολὰς καὶ βλάστας ἔχει 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.68, cf. LXX.Jer.23.5, +others}}

396:
** ἀνα-τρέπω**,
_poetry_ ἀντρ-, _Aeolic dialect_ _aorist_ ὀνέτροπε{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 25.7}}: _perfect_ **-τέτροφα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1009, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.131}} later **-τέτρᾰφα** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.30 codices, variant in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.296, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.190, 3.158:}}_aorist 2_ _middle_ ἀνετράπετο in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.64, 14.447 (only here in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 395d, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 8.90}}: _aorist 2_ _passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 76.3, etc.}}:—**overturn, upset,** _active_, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 56.3, Alc.[same place]; τράπεζαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.198, cf. Scholia ad [same work] 24.136; in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus ἀνετράπετο, ={ὕπτιος ἔπεσεν}, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.64; ἀνατετραμμένος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 543}}]; frequently of ships, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 906e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1398b7, etc.}}; ἂν ἀνατραπῆ γὰρ πλοῖον Alex.[same place]; τὴν σωφροσύνην, τὸν βίον ἅπαντα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.131, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 46.}}
**b** in Medicine texts, **upset,** στόμαχον{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.911}}: so _absolutely_, **create nausea,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 1.12.}}
**2** **overthrow, ruin,** πρόρριζον ἀνατρέψαι τινά{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, compare 8.62; μὴ.. δαίμων.. ἀντρέψῃ ποδὶ ὄλβον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 163; λακπάτητον ἀ. χαράν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1275; πλοῦτον 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.131; πόλιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 671}}; πολιτείαν, οἰκίαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 709a, “R.” 471b; τὰ τῶν Ἑλλήνων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.143}}:—_passive_, ἤρυξε πόλιν μἀνατραπῆναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1082; ὁ βίος ἀνατετραμμένος ἂν εἴη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 481c, etc.}}
**3** **upset** in argument, **refute,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 901; ἀ. πρόβλημα 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Topicorum libros octo commentaria - pars” 514.28.}}
**II** _passive_, **to be upset, disheartened,** ἀνετράπετο φρένα λύπᾳ{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 8.90; ταῖς ψυχαῖς ἀ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.25.8.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_, **to be checked in, diverted from,** ὁρμήν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.15.6; τὴν φιλαργυρίαν 2.14.6.}}
**II.3** **to be turned back,** εἰς χώραν{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.49.68.}}
**II.4** **to be made null and void,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 2.2 “Intr.”}}
**III** **stir up, arouse,** ἀνατέτροφας ὅ τι καὶ μύσῃ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1009}}:—_passive_, of the sea in a storm, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 600a4, etc.}}
**IV** _intransitive_ in _active_, **slip, trip up,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.631c}}; of a ship, **capsize,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 34.32.}}

397:
** ἀνατρέφω**,
**bring up, cherish, educate,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 523, NT.Luke.4.16, +others}}; ἀ. τὸ φρόνημα **raise** the spirit, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.34:}} —_middle_, ἀναθρέψασθαι υἱόν **have** him **educated,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.2.1; ἀ. λειμὼν κάλλεα 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 74.58}}:— _passive_, **grow up,** ἀνατραφῆναι ἐν..{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 34, etc.; τῇ Ἑλλάδι φωνῇ 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 11.25}}; ἀνέτραφες in [{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.156 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus) ={ἀνετράφης}.}}]
**2** **feed up,** opposed to ἰσχναίνω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 33, 50: _metaphorically_, ἀ. μονῳδίαις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 944}}:—_passive_, ἀνατρέφεσθαι ἐκ νόσου **convalesce,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 14}}; of fish after milting, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 608a2.}}

398:
** ἀναφαίνω**,
_poetry_ ἀμφ-: _future_ -φᾰνῶ, but **-φᾱνῶ**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 528 codices (-φαίνω 2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus):}}_aorist_ ἀνέφηνα, Hellenistic -έφᾱνα: _perfect_ -πέφηνα late, Ps.-{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philopatr.” 3}}:—**cause to give light, make to blaze up,** ξύλα, δαΐδας{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.310.}}
**2** **bring to light, produce,** ὄφιας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4. 105.}}
**2.b** **show forth, make known, display,** θεοπροπίας, ἀρετήν, ἐπεσβολίας, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.87, 20.411, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.159; πραπίδων καρπόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 211; κἀνέφηνεν οὐ δεδειγμένα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 432.7; ἀ. θυσίας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 466; ὀργάν [prev. author] “Ba.” 538; ἄστρα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.4; ἡμέρᾳ καὶ ἡλίῳ.. χάριν οἶδα ὅτι μοι Κλεινίαν ἀ. [prev. author] “Smp.” 4.12}}; rarely of sound, βοὰν ἀμφ. **send forth a loud** cry, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 829; ἀ. μελέων νόμους 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 745}}:—in _middle_, νίκαν ἀνεφάνατο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).71.}}
**3** **proclaim, declare,** βασιλέα ἀ. τινά{{Refs|[prev. author] “P.” 4.62}}; νικάσαις ἀνέφανε Κυράναν{{Refs|[prev. work] 9.73, compare “N.” 9.12}}: with _participle_, τοὺς πολίτας ἀγαθοὺς ὄντας ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 108c: with}}_infinitive_, ἀναφαίνω σε τόδε.. ὀνομάζειν I **proclaim that** they call thee by this name, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 528:}} —_middle_, in _Doric dialect_ form ἀμφ-, **adopt** as one's son, {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 10.34, +others}}
**3.b** of things, **appoint, institute,** ὃς τελετὰς ἀνέφηνε καὶ ὄργια{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 3.713, compare “Marm.Par.” 28; νῆσον ἀ. τινὶ οἰκεῖν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 19.16.}}
**4** ἀναφάναντες τὴν Κύπρον **having sighted..,** {{NT|NT.Acts.21.3.}}
**II** _passive_, _future_ ἀναφᾰνήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 950, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 132a, +others}} but also **-φανοῦμαι** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Plt.” 289c:}}_perfect_ ἀναπέφασμαι, but **-πέφηνα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (see. below)}}, etc.: _aorist_ ἀνεφᾰνην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 124}}:—**to be shown forth, appear plainly,** ἀναφαίνεται ἀστήρ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.62}}; ἀ. αἰπὺς ὄλεθρος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 174; τῇ δεκάτῃ.. ἀνεφαίνετο πατρὶς ἄρουρα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.29; τὸ Δέλτα ἐστὶ νεωστὶ ἀναπεφηνός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.15, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1222 (Lyric poetry), etc.; ἀ. ὁ βλάπτων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 328.}}
**II.b** **reappear,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.195}}; of rivers which flow underground, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.76, 7.30}}; simply, **spring up,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 198.}}
**II.2** ἀναφανῆναι μούναρχος **to be declared** king, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.82; στρατηγὸς ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 541e; κλέπτης τις ὁ δίκαιος.. ἀναπέφανται}}**proved to be..,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 334a, compare “Smp.” 185a}}; ἀ. λογογράφος ἐκ τριηράρχου from a sea-captain **to come out** a romancer, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.173}}:—also with _participle_, ἀναπέφανται ὢν ἀγαθός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 350c}}; ἀναφαίνεσθαι ἔχων, σεσωσμένοι, **to be seen** or **found to** have, **to be plainly** in safety, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Sph.” 233c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.15, etc.}}
**III** the _active_ _intransitive_ in later Greek, ἀνέφαινεν ἕσπερος[{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.AD: Musaeus Epicus 111 (variant), compare 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 5.22:—ἀναφῆναι is probably _falsa lectio_ for{ἀναφανῆναι} in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.165.}}]

399:
** ἀναφέρω**,
_poetry_ ἀμφ-, _future_ ἀνοίσω: _aorist_ ἀνήνεγκα, _Ionic dialect_ ἀνήνεικα, also _infinitive_ ἀνοῖσαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.157:}}
**I** **bring, carry up,** [Κέρβερον] ἐξ Ἀΐδαο[{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.625; ἐκ τῆς ἰλύος ψῆγμα ἀ. χρυσοῦ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.195, compare 3.102 (as variant for{-φορέω})}}]; ἀ. τινὰ εἰς Ὄλυμπον, εἰς θεούς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.30 (_passive_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 28, etc.}}; in historical writers, **carry up the country,** especially into Central Asia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.30}}; **raise up,** εἰς τὸ ἄνω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 37}}; ἀ. πόδα **lift** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1410}}:—_middle_, **carry up** to a place of safety, **take with one,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.148}}; **remove one's goods,** {{Refs|8.32, 36, etc.}}
**I.b** especially **carry up to the Acropolis, put by,** of treasure, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 5.12, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.174, etc.}}
**I.2** **bring up, pour forth,** of tears, ἑτοιμότερα γέλωτος ἀ. λίβη{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 447; αἵματος πλῆθος ἀ.}}**spit up,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cleomenes” 15}}; ἀ. φωνάς, στεναγμούς, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.433c, “Alex.” 52:}} —_middle_, ἀνενείκασθαι, _absolutely_, **fetch up a deep-drawn breath, heave a deep sigh,** μνησάμενος δ᾽ ἁδινῶς ἀνενείκατο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.314; ἀνενεικάμενόν τε καὶ ἀναστενάξαντα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86}} (where others, **having recovered himself, come to himself,** see{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|below 11.7): in 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor}} Poets, **utter,** ἀνενείκατο μῦθον, φωνήν, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.463, 635.}}
**I.3** **uphold, take upon one,** ἄχθος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 841; κινδύνους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38}}; διαβολάς, πόλεμον, etc., {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.36.3, 4.45.9, etc.; πολλῶν ἀ. ἁμαρτίας LXX.Isa.53.12, NT.Heb.9.28.}}
**I.4** **offer** in sacrifice, {{Refs|[prev. work]7.27, 13.15, etc.: _absolutely_}}**make expiation** or **compensation**, {{Refs|“GDI” 3537, +others (from Cnidus).}}
**I.5** **raise up, yield,** ἀρχαῖαι ἀρεταὶ ἀμφέροντ᾽ ἀνδρῶν σθένος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 11.38.}}
**I.6** _intransitive_, **lead up,** of a road, ἁμαξιτὸς εἰς τὸν Πειραιᾶ ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.10, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.29.1, “Inscription Prien.” 37.161.}}
**II** **bring** or **carry back,** εἰς τοὔπισθεν ἀ. πόδα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1410: frequently in Prose, ἀ. τὰς κώπας}} **recover** the oars (after pulling them through the water), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.84; ἡ εἰρεσία ἀναφέρεται 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 53, “Ant.” 26.}}
**II.2** **bring back** tidings, **report,** παρά τινα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.47; ἔς τινα [prev. author] 1.91, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.28, etc.}}; τὰ ἐκ τῆς ἐκκλησίας ἀνενεγκόντες Canon Laws texts cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.75}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.141, +others}}
**II.3** **bring back from exile,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.16.}}
**II.4** **carry back, trace** one's family to an ancestor, τὸ Ἡρακλέους γένος εἰς Περσέα ἀναφέρεται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.120e; without γένος, ἀ. εἰς Ἡρακλέα [prev. author] “Tht.” 175a.}}
**II.5** **refer** a matter to another, βουλεύματα ἐς τὸ κοινόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80; ἐς ἀφανὲς τὸν μῦθον ἀ. [prev. author] 2.23; ἁμαρτίαν εἴς τινα ἀ.}}**ascribe** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 76, “Ba.” 29, etc.; τῆς κηλῖδος εἰς ὑμᾶς -ομένης 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.11; τὴν αἰτίαν εἴς τινα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.8; rarely ἀ. τί τινι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 432, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.81; τι ἐπί τινα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.224, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.215; τι ἐπί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 76d; τι πρός τι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1101b19 (_passive_), +others}}; ποῖ δίκην ἀνοίσομε; to whom **shall** we **refer** the judgement? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 253; τὴν ἀπόδοσιν εἴς τινα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.46}}:—_passive_, **to be attributed** (of authorship), εἰς Μητρόδωρον{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1005.8}}; **to be traced to, derived from,** ἐπί τι {{Refs|[prev. work]1251.11.}}
**II.6** _passive_, **refer to,** of a statement, πρός τι Ps.-{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor Aphr. “in SE” 127.8.}}
**II.6.b** without _accusative_, ἀ. εἴς τινα **refer** or **appeal** to another, **make reference** to him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 20e; ἔς τινα περί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.157, 7.149}}; ἀ. πρός τι **refer** to something as to a standard, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 9; ἐκεῖσε ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 484c, compare “Phdr.” 237d.}}
**II.6.c** **report,** μέτρα καὶ γειτνίας καὶ ἀξίας{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 14.11 (2nd c.BC), etc.:}} —_passive_ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 10.3 (2nd c.BC)}}: _absolutely_, **make a report,** τινί{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 233.8 (2nd c.AD), “PFay.” 129.8 (3rd c.AD).}}
